n-known"]}, {"id": "ci-misconception", "type": "callout", "title": "Where the 95% belongs", "text": "Once the numerical interval is observed, it either contains the fixed mean or it does not. The 95% refers to the performance of the interval-generating procedure over repeated samples. A probability distribution for \u03bc after seeing data would require a different modelling framework; it does not follow from this frequentist calculation.", "tone": "warning", "objectives": ["3.2.1"]}, {"id": "ci-example", "type": "worked", "title": "A processing-cost interval", "objectives": ["3.2.3.mean-known"], "steps": [{"title": "State the inputs", "text": "For an independent normal sample of n = 25 costs, suppose x\u0304 = 104 and the known population standard deviation is 20. Use 95% confidence.", "why": "The known population standard deviation is an assumption, not a value estimated from these 25 records."}, {"title": "Compute the standard error", "text": "20 / \u221a25 = 4 currency units. The 0.975 normal quantile is about 1.959964.", "why": "Each tail has 0.025 probability under the standard normal model."}, {"title": "Form the interval", "text": "The margin is 1.959964 \u00d7 4 = 7.839856. The interval is [96.160144, 111.839856], or [96.16, 111.84] to two decimal places.", "why": "The endpoints, not just the centre, must use consistent units and rounding."}, {"title": "Interpret and check", "text": "This is one interval generated by a procedure with 95% coverage under the stated model. Its length is 15.679712 currency units.", "why": "The interval does not contain 95% of individual costs; it targets a population mean."}]}, {"id": "ci-calculate", "type": "question", "prompt": "An independent normal sample has observed mean 52, known population standard deviation 12, and n = 36. Using z = 1.96, what is the lower endpoint of the 95% confidence interval?", "solution": "Standard error = 12/\u221a36 = 2. Margin = 1.96\u00d72 = 3.92. The lower endpoint is 52\u22123.92 = 48.08. The corresponding upper endpoint is 55.92.", "taughtIn": ["ci-formula", "ci-example"], "objectives": ["3.2.2.normal-known", "3.2.3.mean-known"], "questionKind": "numeric", "answer": 48.08, "tolerance": 0.005, "unit": "currency units", "hints": ["Find the standard error first.", "A lower endpoint subtracts the margin from the observed mean."]}, {"id": "ci-reconstruct", "type": "question", "questionKind": "written", "objectives": ["3.2.2.normal-known"], "prompt": "For independent normal observations with unknown mean \u03bc and known \u03c3>0, derive a two-sided (1\u2212\u03b1) confidence interval for \u03bc. Start from the standard normal pivot, show the inequality rearrangement, and identify which quantities are random before sampling.", "taughtIn": ["ci-model", "ci-pivot", "ci-derivation", "ci-formula"], "solution": "Under the model, Z=(X\u0304\u2212\u03bc)/(\u03c3/\u221an) has a standard normal distribution. Put z=z\u2081\u208b\u03b1/\u2082. Then P(\u2212z\u2264(X\u0304\u2212\u03bc)/(\u03c3/\u221an)\u2264z)=1\u2212\u03b1. Multiplying by the positive standard error gives the equivalent event \u2212z\u03c3/\u221an\u2264X\u0304\u2212\u03bc\u2264z\u03c3/\u221an. Rearranging yields X\u0304\u2212z\u03c3/\u221an\u2264\u03bc\u2264X\u0304+z\u03c3/\u221an. Therefore the two endpoints define a random interval with coverage 1\u2212\u03b1 under the model. The endpoints depend on X\u0304 and are random before sampling; \u03bc is fixed. Observing x\u0304 yields numerical endpoints without assigning a frequentist posterior probability to \u03bc.", "rubric": [{"text": "Specify the standard normal pivot and its model assumptions.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["ci-model", "ci-pivot"]}, {"text": "Allocate \u03b1/2 to each tail and state the central probability event.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["ci-pivot", "ci-derivation"]}, {"text": "Rearrange using the positive scale to obtain both correct endpoints.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["ci-derivation", "ci-formula"]}, {"text": "Identify the random endpoints and fixed parameter before sampling.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["ci-model", "ci-derivation"]}]}, {"id": "ci-width-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "Coverage and precision answer different questions", "level": 2}, {"id": "ci-width", "type": "prose", "text": "The interval length is $2z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\sigma/\\sqrt n$. With the same \u03c3 and confidence level, increasing n from 25 to 100 halves the length. Increasing the confidence level instead requires a larger quantile, so it widens the interval. A narrower interval is not automatically a better statement if it achieves that narrowness by reducing coverage or ignoring important uncertainty.\n\nFor our exact model, a 99% procedure covers the mean more often in repeated samples than a 90% procedure, at the cost of wider intervals. Neither procedure makes a biased sampling design representative. If \u03c3 is unknown, replacing it with a sample standard deviation changes the pivot; the appropriate t method is outside this unit.", "objectives": ["3.2.1", "3.2.3.mean-known"]}, {"id": "coverage-lab", "type": "lab", "labKind": "coverage", "title": "Watch the interval move, not the parameter", "objectives": ["3.2.1", "3.2.3.mean-known"], "purpose": "Separate the fixed true mean from the random intervals created by repeated normal samples.", "prediction": "Predict how many of 50 independently generated 95% intervals will miss the true mean. Must it be exactly two or three?", "debrief": "The miss count is itself random. A finite batch need not achieve exactly the nominal coverage. The fixed vertical line marks the true mean; endpoints move because samples change.", "fallback": "With known \u03c3=20 and n=25, every 95% interval has theoretical length about 15.68. Repeating the procedure 50 times produces an expected 47.5 covering intervals, not a guaranteed count. The true mean does not move between repetitions.", "transfer": "Explain what would happen to interval length and long-run coverage if n quadrupled but the nominal confidence level stayed fixed."}, {"id": "ci-interpret", "type": "question", "prompt": "After computing [96.16, 111.84], which statement matches the frequentist model?", "solution": "The interval is one realised output of a method with 95% repeated-sampling coverage under the model. Individual costs are not its target, and no posterior probability for the fixed mean has been specified.", "taughtIn": ["ci-model", "ci-derivation", "ci-misconception", "coverage-lab"], "objectives": ["3.2.1"], "questionKind": "choice", "options": ["95% of individual processing costs lie inside these endpoints.", "The fixed mean has a 95% frequentist probability of being inside these now-fixed endpoints.", "The interval came from a procedure that covers the fixed mean in 95% of repeated samples under the model."], "correct": 2, "feedback": ["A confidence interval for a mean does not estimate an interval containing 95% of individuals.", "That probability interpretation would require an additional model for the parameter; it is not this calculation.", "Yes. Coverage describes the procedure before observing a particular sample."]}, {"id": "ci-transfer", "type": "question", "prompt": "A manager wants both shorter intervals and higher confidence. Explain why changing confidence alone cannot achieve both, and identify one model-preserving way to improve precision.", "solution": "Higher confidence increases the quantile and therefore widens the interval at fixed n and \u03c3. Increasing the size of a genuinely independent representative sample reduces standard error and can offset that width increase. It does not repair selection bias.", "taughtIn": ["ci-width", "ci-model"], "objectives": ["3.2.1", "3.2.3.mean-known"], "questionKind": "written", "rubric": [{"text": "Link higher confidence to a larger quantile and greater width.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["ci-width"]}, {"text": "Explain how increasing a valid sample size reduces standard error, without claiming bias is fixed.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["ci-width", "ci-model"]}]}, {"id": "ci-readiness", "type": "callout", "title": "Choose a repair, then retry", "tone": "idea", "text": "If you described 95% of individual costs, return to \u201cWhere the 95% belongs\u201d. If you assigned 95% frequentist probability to the fixed mean after observing endpoints, revisit the random-endpoint derivation and coverage lab. If higher confidence made your interval narrower, revisit \u201cCoverage and precision answer different questions\u201d. Retry without the displayed formula: independent normal data have known \u03c3=10, n=25 and observed mean 50. Using z=1.96 gives SE=2 and interval [46.08,53.92]. Explain why quadrupling n halves the width while leaving the model\u2019s nominal coverage at 95%. Reading these endpoints is not evidence that you can reconstruct their derivation."}], "summary": ["A confidence interval is a random procedure before sampling and fixed endpoints after observation.", "The known-variance normal mean interval follows by rearranging a standard normal pivot.", "More confidence widens an interval; larger independent samples narrow it.", "The advertised coverage depends on the model and sampling plan."], "coverage": [{"objective": "3.2.1", "teaching": ["ci-model", "ci-misconception"], "practice": ["ci-interpret", "ci-transfer"]}, {"objective": "3.2.2.normal-known", "teaching": ["ci-pivot", "ci-derivation"], "practice": ["ci-calculate", "ci-reconstruct"]}, {"objective": "3.2.3.mean-known", "teaching": ["ci-formula", "ci-example", "ci-width"], "practice": ["ci-calculate", "ci-transfer"]}], "provenance": "Original prose, fictional situations, numerical designs, questions and interaction brief created independently for Open Actuary. The supplied 2019 syllabus identifies scope; no ActEd text, fictional case, exercise or diagram is reproduced.", "technicalNotes": ["Normal quantiles and endpoints are checked against independent reference values.", "Simulation counts are stochastic illustrations; the coverage probability follows from the pivot.", "Known variance and exact normality are maintained in this unit; no t or bootstrap claim is made."]}','2026-10-27T16:40:35.445Z','Migrated exact published snapshot from original Site');
INSERT INTO documents(id,kind,course_id,draft,revision,published,published_revision,review,updated_at,published_review) VALUES('sampling-means','lesson','cs1-inference','{"id": "sampling-means", "courseId": "cs1-inference", "title": "A sample mean is a moving target", "subtitle": "One population. Many possible samples. A distribution of answers.", "minutes": 30, "prerequisites": ["Expectation, variance, independence and the shape of a normal distribution.", "Summing finite sequences and interpreting a probability density."], "outcomes": ["Separate individual variability from variability of a mean.", "Derive standard error and explain what changes with sample size.", "Use simulation to challenge, rather than replace, the assumptions of the central limit theorem."], "objectiveIds": ["2.2.1", "2.2.2", "2.2.3", "2.2.4.mean", "1.5.1", "1.5.2"], "scopeNote": "Selected historical CS1 scope only. Sample-variance distributions, t and F methods are deferred.", "blocks": [{"id": "opening-question", "type": "callout", "title": "Before we calculate", "text": "An insurer cannot examine every administration transaction. It samples 25 records and estimates the average processing cost. If another team takes a different sample, should it obtain the same answer? The population can stay unchanged while the estimate moves.", "tone": "idea", "objectives": []}, {"id": "population-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "The object that stays fixed, and the object that changes", "level": 2}, {"id": "population-story", "type": "prose", "text": "The population is the collection or probability model about which we want to learn. A sample is the observed part we use. Statistical inference uses the sample to make a statement about a population quantity, such as its mean. A parameter belongs to the population model; a statistic is a function of the sample.\n\nLet $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be processing costs from an independent random sample with common mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$. Before observation, these symbols denote random variables. After sampling, their realised values are numbers. The independence assumption says that knowing one cost does not alter the distribution of another. It is a substantive assumption about selection and dependence, not a reward for collecting many records.", "objectives": ["2.2.1", "2.2.2", "2.2.3"]}, {"id": "sampling-warning", "type": "callout", "title": "A large sample can repeat a selection error", "text": "If only transactions completed during quiet shifts are sampled, the result may not represent all shifts. More records from the same selected group can shrink sampling variation while leaving the selection problem. The calculations below assume the sample really comes from the stated common population.", "tone": "warning", "objectives": ["2.2.2"]}, {"id": "distribution-story", "type": "prose", "text": "The sample mean is $\\bar X=(X_1+\\cdots+X_n)/n$. Once we see the sample, its value is a number. Before we see it, it is random because it is calculated from random inputs. Imagine repeating the same sampling plan many times without changing the population. The distribution of the resulting means is the sampling distribution of $\\bar X$.\n\nThis is a different distribution from that of individual costs. A histogram of costs tells us how transactions differ. A histogram of means tells us how a particular estimation procedure differs between repeated samples. A histogram from one observed sample is neither a census of the population nor, by itself, a sampling distribution.", "objectives": ["2.2.3"]}, {"id": "objects-check", "type": "question", "prompt": "A team draws 40 new records every week and plots each week\u2019s mean cost. If the population and sampling plan remain unchanged, which distribution is the plot trying to approximate?", "solution": "It approximates the sampling distribution of the mean for samples of size 40. Each plotted point is an entire sample summarised by one statistic, not one transaction.", "taughtIn": ["distribution-story"], "objectives": ["2.2.1", "2.2.3"], "questionKind": "choice", "options": ["The distribution of individual transaction costs", "The sampling distribution of the sample mean", "The probability distribution of a fixed population parameter"], "correct": 1, "feedback": ["Each point is a mean of 40 records, not an individual record.", "Yes. Repeating the sampling procedure makes the statistic vary.", "The parameter is fixed in this model; our statistic is random."]}, {"id": "derive-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "Why averaging changes spread", "level": 2}, {"id": "covariance-bridge", "type": "prose", "text": "Covariance describes how two random deviations move together. For finite second moments, Cov(X,Y)=E[(X\u2212E[X])(Y\u2212E[Y])]. A positive value means same-sign deviations contribute more on average than opposite-sign deviations; independence makes covariance zero, although zero covariance alone does not establish independence. Covariance has the product of the two variables\u2019 units.\n\nTo see why it enters, write U=X\u2212E[X] and V=Y\u2212E[Y]. Then Var(X+Y)=E[(U+V)\u00b2]=E[U\u00b2]+2E[UV]+E[V\u00b2]=Var(X)+2Cov(X,Y)+Var(Y). Each pair of different variables contributes twice when the squared sum is expanded. Dividing a sum by n scales its variance by 1/n\u00b2. These are the two ingredients used next.\n\nBoundary check: suppose X\u2081=\u22ef=X\u2099=Y, all copies of the same random outcome with variance \u03c3\u00b2>0. Then X\u0304=Y and Var(X\u0304)=\u03c3\u00b2, regardless of n. The covariance formula agrees: every distinct pair has covariance \u03c3\u00b2, giving [n\u03c3\u00b2+n(n\u22121)\u03c3\u00b2]/n\u00b2=\u03c3\u00b2. A larger row count cannot create independent information.", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"]}, {"id": "mean-derivation", "type": "prose", "text": "Linearity of expectation gives $E[\\bar X]=(E[X_1]+\\cdots+E[X_n])/n=\\mu$. Independence is not needed for this equality when the common mean exists. For variance, multiplying a variable by $1/n$ multiplies its variance by $1/n^2$. Independence removes covariance terms, so $\\operatorname{Var}(X_1+\\cdots+X_n)=n\\sigma^2$. Combining the two steps gives the formula below.\n\nIf the observations are positively correlated, omitted covariance terms can make the actual variance larger. It is therefore wrong to use the independent-sample formula merely because a sample contains n rows.\n\nIn general, Var(\u2211X\u1d62)=\u2211Var(X\u1d62)+2\u2211\u1d62<\u2c7c Cov(X\u1d62,X\u2c7c): the second sum includes each distinct pair once, with a factor of two. Combining this with the scale 1/n\u00b2 gives the correlated-sample expression. Independence sets the pairwise covariances to zero, rather than changing linearity of expectation.", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"]}, {"id": "se-formula", "type": "math", "tex": "E[\\bar X]=\\mu,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n},\\qquad \\operatorname{SD}(\\bar X)=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}", "explanation": "A mean stays centred on the population mean, while its standard deviation decreases with the square root of sample size. This standard deviation is called its standard error.", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"]}, {"id": "se-example", "type": "worked", "title": "How much information does a larger sample buy?", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"], "steps": [{"title": "Define the model", "text": "Suppose individual processing costs have mean 100 and standard deviation 20 currency units, and observations are independent.", "why": "Individual standard deviation describes cost variability, not uncertainty in a sample mean."}, {"title": "Calculate the first standard error", "text": "For n = 25, standard error = 20 / \u221a25 = 4 currency units.", "why": "Taking a mean averages some individual variation away."}, {"title": "Compare, rather than memorise", "text": "For n = 100, standard error = 20 / \u221a100 = 2. Four times as many observations halves the standard error.", "why": "To halve 1/\u221an, multiply n by four. Doubling n only multiplies the standard error by 1/\u221a2."}, {"title": "Check meaning and units", "text": "Both the mean and standard error are in currency units. The mean is still centred at 100. Larger n changes precision, not the population parameter.", "why": "A more precise estimate is not automatically unbiased if the sample design is wrong."}]}, {"id": "se-practice", "type": "question", "prompt": "Individual costs have standard deviation 18. What is the standard error of an independent sample mean based on 81 records? Give the answer in currency units.", "solution": "The standard error is 18/\u221a81 = 2. The variance is 4, which has squared units and is a different answer.", "taughtIn": ["mean-derivation", "se-formula", "se-example"], "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"], "questionKind": "numeric", "answer": 2, "tolerance": 0.001, "unit": "currency units", "hints": ["The question asks for standard deviation of the mean, not its variance.", "Use the square root of the sample size in the denominator."]}, {"id": "mean-reconstruct", "type": "question", "questionKind": "written", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"], "prompt": "Without looking at the displayed formula, reconstruct E[X\u0304] and Var(X\u0304) for n observations with common mean \u03bc and variance \u03c3\u00b2. State exactly where independence enters, and show what additional term appears when the observations have nonzero covariances.", "taughtIn": ["covariance-bridge", "mean-derivation", "se-formula"], "solution": "Write X\u0304=(1/n)\u2211X\u1d62. Linearity gives E[X\u0304]=(1/n)\u2211E[X\u1d62]=\u03bc; independence is unnecessary for that step. The variance of a scaled variable is multiplied by the square of the scale. Hence Var(X\u0304)=(1/n\u00b2)[\u2211Var(X\u1d62)+2\u2211\u1d62<\u2c7c Cov(X\u1d62,X\u2c7c)]. With common variance this is \u03c3\u00b2/n+(2/n\u00b2)\u2211\u1d62<\u2c7c Cov(X\u1d62,X\u2c7c). Independence makes every covariance zero, leaving \u03c3\u00b2/n. These identities explain why a large correlated sample need not have the independent-sample standard error. As a check on the general expression, identical copies X\u1d62=Y give Var(X\u0304)=\u03c3\u00b2, because the n(n\u22121)/2 equal covariance terms exactly supply the variation omitted by an independence assumption.", "rubric": [{"text": "Construct X\u0304=(1/n)\u2211X\u1d62 and derive its expectation \u03bc by linearity, which needs no independence.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["mean-derivation"]}, {"text": "Use the squared scale 1/n\u00b2 when taking the variance of the sum.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["covariance-bridge", "mean-derivation"]}, {"text": "Expand the sum variance into n\u03c3\u00b2 plus twice the sum of distinct-pair covariances.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["covariance-bridge", "mean-derivation"]}, {"text": "Specialize the variance expression to independent observations by setting covariances to zero and simplify to \u03c3\u00b2/n.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["mean-derivation", "se-formula"]}]}, {"id": "clt-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "When does the shape become normal?", "level": 2}, {"id": "clt-statement", "type": "prose", "text": "For independent identically distributed observations with finite mean and finite nonzero variance, the standardised mean $\\sqrt n(\\bar X-\\mu)/\\sigma$ converges in distribution to a standard normal as n increases. This is the iid central limit theorem. It concerns the distribution of a statistic across samples, not a promise that individual observations become normal.\n\nIf the population itself is normal, the sample mean is exactly normal for every n. For a skewed population, normality of the mean is an approximation whose quality depends on the distribution and the region of interest. There is no universal sample size that makes every tail probability accurate. Strong dependence or infinite variance can invalidate the theorem stated here.", "objectives": ["1.5.1"]}, {"id": "clt-reconstruct", "type": "question", "questionKind": "written", "prompt": "Without the displayed theorem, state the iid central limit theorem for a sample mean: specify the assumptions, the standardized random variable and its limiting distribution. Explain whether it makes individual observations normal, whether it is exact for a finite sample from a skewed population, and why a population with infinite variance is outside this theorem. Finally distinguish the normal-population case. [7 marks]", "solution": "For independent identically distributed X\u2081,\u2026,X\u2099 with finite common mean \u03bc and finite nonzero variance \u03c3\u00b2, \u221an(X\u0304\u2212\u03bc)/\u03c3 converges in distribution to N(0,1) as n\u2192\u221e. [2 assumptions; 2 standardized variable and limiting law.] This concerns the sampling distribution of a mean, not the distribution of individual observations. For a skewed population the finite-n normal approximation is not exact, with accuracy depending on the population and probability region. [1] Infinite variance violates the finite-variance hypothesis, so this stated theorem cannot justify the approximation. [1] If the population is normal, X\u0304 itself has the exact N(\u03bc,\u03c3\u00b2/n) distribution at every n, without waiting for a limit. [1]", "taughtIn": ["distribution-story", "clt-statement"], "objectives": ["1.5.1"], "rubric": [{"text": "State iid observations, finite mean and finite nonzero variance.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Give \u221an(X\u0304\u2212\u03bc)/\u03c3 and convergence in distribution to N(0,1) as n\u2192\u221e.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Distinguish individual observations from repeated-sample means and identify finite-n normality as approximate for a skewed population.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["distribution-story", "clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Identify infinite variance as failure of a stated hypothesis.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Distinguish exact normality of the mean for a normal population at every n.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["clt-statement"]}]}, {"id": "sampling-lab", "type": "lab", "labKind": "sampling", "title": "Build a distribution of means", "objectives": ["1.5.1", "1.5.2", "2.2.3", "2.2.4.mean"], "purpose": "Compare the population shape with the variability and approximate shape of repeated sample means.", "prediction": "Before drawing, predict what happens to the centre, spread and skew when sample size changes from 4 to 64 for an exponential population.", "debrief": "The empirical histogram is finite simulation evidence. Compare its spread with \u03c3/\u221an. The normal overlay is a theoretical reference, not a fit guaranteed by a smooth-looking histogram. Use Compare spread for a shared horizontal scale. Then choose Inspect shape at each n to examine the normal reference; this view changes the horizontal scale, so its widths cannot be compared across n.", "fallback": "For an exponential population with mean and standard deviation 10, the theoretical mean of every sample mean is 10. Its standard error is 5 at n=4 and 1.25 at n=64. Averaging reduces right skew, although finite-n means are not exactly normal.", "transfer": "Would taking 64 neighbouring correlated records justify the same standard error? Explain which step of the variance argument would change."}, {"id": "simulation-reporting", "type": "prose", "text": "Report a simulation as an observation, not as a theorem: name the population, n, number of repetitions and seed, then compare the empirical standard deviation of the generated means with \u03c3/\u221an. If the lab does not display the seed or repetition count, say that those settings were not exposed; do not invent them. Compare the histogram with the normal reference and describe what you actually see. Finite empirical spread need not equal the theoretical value.\n\nThe following is an actual separate reference computation, not a run of the on-screen lab. Algorithm: Python random.Random(2710), reset to that seed for each n; draw independent exponential values with random.expovariate(0.1), average n values, repeat 2,000 times, and compute empirical standard deviation with divisor 1,999 (statistics.stdev). For n=4, the empirical centre was 10.269042 and spread 5.051620, versus theoretical centre 10 and spread 5. For n=64, they were 10.005911 and 1.229425, versus 10 and 1.25. The differences are finite-simulation variation. These computed summaries do not themselves establish a histogram shape or validate real-world sampling assumptions; inspect the lab plot separately. Other algorithms, seeds or repetition counts can produce different values.", "objectives": ["1.5.2"]}, {"id": "clt-transfer", "type": "question", "prompt": "First use the sampling lab to generate exponential sample means at n=4 and n=64. Use Compare spread to compare their horizontal spread, then Inspect shape at each n to examine the normal reference on its fitted scale. Record each empirical spread and any displayed simulation seed/repetition count (say if unavailable), compare each with its theoretical standard error, and comment on the normal reference shape without treating one finite histogram as proof. Then a colleague says: \u201cAt n = 100 all individual costs are approximately normal, and the variance of the mean must be \u03c3\u00b2/100 even if the records are correlated.\u201d Identify two separate errors.", "solution": "An honest report gives the two empirical spreads actually observed, settings where available, comparison with theoretical standard errors 5 and 1.25, and a description of the visible shapes. As a labelled computed example, the separate reference computation in the reporting example above used random.Random(2710), reset for each n, exponential rate 0.1 and 2,000 repetitions: spreads were 5.051620 at n=4 and 1.229425 at n=64. These are example results, not required lab outputs. Reproduce the comparison with your own run; describe its actual histogram rather than copying an expected shape. The n=64 means are typically less skewed, but one finite plot is not proof. The colleague\u2019s first error confuses a sampling distribution with individual costs. The second discards covariance contributions: independence removes them from the sum variance, and a large n does not do so automatically.", "taughtIn": ["distribution-story", "mean-derivation", "clt-statement", "sampling-lab", "simulation-reporting"], "objectives": ["1.5.1", "1.5.2", "2.2.2"], "questionKind": "written", "rubric": [{"text": "Report both actual empirical spreads and available settings, compare with theoretical spread and the observed normal-reference shape, and acknowledge finite simulation variation.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["sampling-lab", "clt-statement", "simulation-reporting"]}, {"text": "Distinguish individual observations from the sampling distribution of a mean.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["distribution-story", "clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Explain the independence/covariance issue rather than relying on sample size.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["mean-derivation"]}]}, {"id": "sampling-readiness", "type": "callout", "title": "Choose a repair, then retry", "tone": "idea", "text": "If you used \u03c3\u00b2/n as a standard error, return to \u201cWhy averaging changes spread\u201d and keep variance in squared units. If you removed covariance merely because n is large, revisit the identical-copy check in the covariance bridge. If you made individual observations normal, revisit \u201cWhen does the shape become normal?\u201d Then retry without the formula: with \u03c3=15 and n=25, explain why the independent-mean standard error is 3, whereas identical copies have standard error 15. State the CLT assumptions aloud before using any normal approximation. Revealing feedback or moving controls does not establish mastery."}], "summary": ["A statistic is random before data are observed; its realised value is a number.", "For an iid finite-variance model, the sample mean has mean \u03bc and variance \u03c3\u00b2/n.", "The central limit theorem describes repeated-sample means, with assumptions and approximation limits.", "Simulation helps inspect a model; it does not verify that the real sampling design satisfies the model."], "coverage": [{"objective": "2.2.1", "teaching": ["population-story"], "practice": ["objects-check"]}, {"objective": "2.2.2", "teaching": ["population-story", "sampling-warning"], "practice": ["clt-transfer"]}, {"objective": "2.2.3", "teaching": ["distribution-story"], "practice": ["objects-check"]}, {"objective": "2.2.4.mean", "teaching": ["covariance-bridge", "mean-derivation", "se-formula"], "practice": ["se-practice", "mean-reconstruct"]}, {"objective": "1.5.1", "teaching": ["clt-statement"], "practice": ["clt-reconstruct", "clt-transfer"]}, {"objective": "1.5.2", "teaching": ["sampling-lab", "simulation-reporting"], "practice": ["clt-transfer"]}], "provenance": "Original prose, fictional situations, numerical designs, questions and interaction brief created independently for Open Actuary. The supplied 2019 syllabus identifies scope; no ActEd text, fictional case, exercise or diagram is reproduced.", "technicalNotes": ["Known population moments are model inputs; the finite simulation is generated with a reproducible seed.", "Mean/variance formulas can be checked independently by linearity and covariance expansion.", "Normal approximation and standard-error calculations are separated; exact normality requires a normal population."]}','5','{"id": "sampling-means", "courseId": "cs1-inference", "title": "A sample mean is a moving target", "subtitle": "One population. Many possible samples. A distribution of answers.", "minutes": 30, "prerequisites": ["Expectation, variance, independence and the shape of a normal distribution.", "Summing finite sequences and interpreting a probability density."], "outcomes": ["Separate individual variability from variability of a mean.", "Derive standard error and explain what changes with sample size.", "Use simulation to challenge, rather than replace, the assumptions of the central limit theorem."], "objectiveIds": ["2.2.1", "2.2.2", "2.2.3", "2.2.4.mean", "1.5.1", "1.5.2"], "scopeNote": "Selected historical CS1 scope only. Sample-variance distributions, t and F methods are deferred.", "blocks": [{"id": "opening-question", "type": "callout", "title": "Before we calculate", "text": "An insurer cannot examine every administration transaction. It samples 25 records and estimates the average processing cost. If another team takes a different sample, should it obtain the same answer? The population can stay unchanged while the estimate moves.", "tone": "idea", "objectives": []}, {"id": "population-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "The object that stays fixed, and the object that changes", "level": 2}, {"id": "population-story", "type": "prose", "text": "The population is the collection or probability model about which we want to learn. A sample is the observed part we use. Statistical inference uses the sample to make a statement about a population quantity, such as its mean. A parameter belongs to the population model; a statistic is a function of the sample.\n\nLet $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be processing costs from an independent random sample with common mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$. Before observation, these symbols denote random variables. After sampling, their realised values are numbers. The independence assumption says that knowing one cost does not alter the distribution of another. It is a substantive assumption about selection and dependence, not a reward for collecting many records.", "objectives": ["2.2.1", "2.2.2", "2.2.3"]}, {"id": "sampling-warning", "type": "callout", "title": "A large sample can repeat a selection error", "text": "If only transactions completed during quiet shifts are sampled, the result may not represent all shifts. More records from the same selected group can shrink sampling variation while leaving the selection problem. The calculations below assume the sample really comes from the stated common population.", "tone": "warning", "objectives": ["2.2.2"]}, {"id": "distribution-story", "type": "prose", "text": "The sample mean is $\\bar X=(X_1+\\cdots+X_n)/n$. Once we see the sample, its value is a number. Before we see it, it is random because it is calculated from random inputs. Imagine repeating the same sampling plan many times without changing the population. The distribution of the resulting means is the sampling distribution of $\\bar X$.\n\nThis is a different distribution from that of individual costs. A histogram of costs tells us how transactions differ. A histogram of means tells us how a particular estimation procedure differs between repeated samples. A histogram from one observed sample is neither a census of the population nor, by itself, a sampling distribution.", "objectives": ["2.2.3"]}, {"id": "objects-check", "type": "question", "prompt": "A team draws 40 new records every week and plots each week\u2019s mean cost. If the population and sampling plan remain unchanged, which distribution is the plot trying to approximate?", "solution": "It approximates the sampling distribution of the mean for samples of size 40. Each plotted point is an entire sample summarised by one statistic, not one transaction.", "taughtIn": ["distribution-story"], "objectives": ["2.2.1", "2.2.3"], "questionKind": "choice", "options": ["The distribution of individual transaction costs", "The sampling distribution of the sample mean", "The probability distribution of a fixed population parameter"], "correct": 1, "feedback": ["Each point is a mean of 40 records, not an individual record.", "Yes. Repeating the sampling procedure makes the statistic vary.", "The parameter is fixed in this model; our statistic is random."]}, {"id": "derive-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "Why averaging changes spread", "level": 2}, {"id": "covariance-bridge", "type": "prose", "text": "Covariance describes how two random deviations move together. For finite second moments, Cov(X,Y)=E[(X\u2212E[X])(Y\u2212E[Y])]. A positive value means same-sign deviations contribute more on average than opposite-sign deviations; independence makes covariance zero, although zero covariance alone does not establish independence. Covariance has the product of the two variables\u2019 units.\n\nTo see why it enters, write U=X\u2212E[X] and V=Y\u2212E[Y]. Then Var(X+Y)=E[(U+V)\u00b2]=E[U\u00b2]+2E[UV]+E[V\u00b2]=Var(X)+2Cov(X,Y)+Var(Y). Each pair of different variables contributes twice when the squared sum is expanded. Dividing a sum by n scales its variance by 1/n\u00b2. These are the two ingredients used next.\n\nBoundary check: suppose X\u2081=\u22ef=X\u2099=Y, all copies of the same random outcome with variance \u03c3\u00b2>0. Then X\u0304=Y and Var(X\u0304)=\u03c3\u00b2, regardless of n. The covariance formula agrees: every distinct pair has covariance \u03c3\u00b2, giving [n\u03c3\u00b2+n(n\u22121)\u03c3\u00b2]/n\u00b2=\u03c3\u00b2. A larger row count cannot create independent information.", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"]}, {"id": "mean-derivation", "type": "prose", "text": "Linearity of expectation gives $E[\\bar X]=(E[X_1]+\\cdots+E[X_n])/n=\\mu$. Independence is not needed for this equality when the common mean exists. For variance, multiplying a variable by $1/n$ multiplies its variance by $1/n^2$. Independence removes covariance terms, so $\\operatorname{Var}(X_1+\\cdots+X_n)=n\\sigma^2$. Combining the two steps gives the formula below.\n\nIf the observations are positively correlated, omitted covariance terms can make the actual variance larger. It is therefore wrong to use the independent-sample formula merely because a sample contains n rows.\n\nIn general, Var(\u2211X\u1d62)=\u2211Var(X\u1d62)+2\u2211\u1d62<\u2c7c Cov(X\u1d62,X\u2c7c): the second sum includes each distinct pair once, with a factor of two. Combining this with the scale 1/n\u00b2 gives the correlated-sample expression. Independence sets the pairwise covariances to zero, rather than changing linearity of expectation.", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"]}, {"id": "se-formula", "type": "math", "tex": "E[\\bar X]=\\mu,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n},\\qquad \\operatorname{SD}(\\bar X)=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}", "explanation": "A mean stays centred on the population mean, while its standard deviation decreases with the square root of sample size. This standard deviation is called its standard error.", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"]}, {"id": "se-example", "type": "worked", "title": "How much information does a larger sample buy?", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"], "steps": [{"title": "Define the model", "text": "Suppose individual processing costs have mean 100 and standard deviation 20 currency units, and observations are independent.", "why": "Individual standard deviation describes cost variability, not uncertainty in a sample mean."}, {"title": "Calculate the first standard error", "text": "For n = 25, standard error = 20 / \u221a25 = 4 currency units.", "why": "Taking a mean averages some individual variation away."}, {"title": "Compare, rather than memorise", "text": "For n = 100, standard error = 20 / \u221a100 = 2. Four times as many observations halves the standard error.", "why": "To halve 1/\u221an, multiply n by four. Doubling n only multiplies the standard error by 1/\u221a2."}, {"title": "Check meaning and units", "text": "Both the mean and standard error are in currency units. The mean is still centred at 100. Larger n changes precision, not the population parameter.", "why": "A more precise estimate is not automatically unbiased if the sample design is wrong."}]}, {"id": "se-practice", "type": "question", "prompt": "Individual costs have standard deviation 18. What is the standard error of an independent sample mean based on 81 records? Give the answer in currency units.", "solution": "The standard error is 18/\u221a81 = 2. The variance is 4, which has squared units and is a different answer.", "taughtIn": ["mean-derivation", "se-formula", "se-example"], "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"], "questionKind": "numeric", "answer": 2, "tolerance": 0.001, "unit": "currency units", "hints": ["The question asks for standard deviation of the mean, not its variance.", "Use the square root of the sample size in the denominator."]}, {"id": "mean-reconstruct", "type": "question", "questionKind": "written", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"], "prompt": "Without looking at the displayed formula, reconstruct E[X\u0304] and Var(X\u0304) for n observations with common mean \u03bc and variance \u03c3\u00b2. State exactly where independence enters, and show what additional term appears when the observations have nonzero covariances.", "taughtIn": ["covariance-bridge", "mean-derivation", "se-formula"], "solution": "Write X\u0304=(1/n)\u2211X\u1d62. Linearity gives E[X\u0304]=(1/n)\u2211E[X\u1d62]=\u03bc; independence is unnecessary for that step. The variance of a scaled variable is multiplied by the square of the scale. Hence Var(X\u0304)=(1/n\u00b2)[\u2211Var(X\u1d62)+2\u2211\u1d62<\u2c7c Cov(X\u1d62,X\u2c7c)]. With common variance this is \u03c3\u00b2/n+(2/n\u00b2)\u2211\u1d62<\u2c7c Cov(X\u1d62,X\u2c7c). Independence makes every covariance zero, leaving \u03c3\u00b2/n. These identities explain why a large correlated sample need not have the independent-sample standard error. As a check on the general expression, identical copies X\u1d62=Y give Var(X\u0304)=\u03c3\u00b2, because the n(n\u22121)/2 equal covariance terms exactly supply the variation omitted by an independence assumption.", "rubric": [{"text": "Construct X\u0304=(1/n)\u2211X\u1d62 and derive its expectation \u03bc by linearity, which needs no independence.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["mean-derivation"]}, {"text": "Use the squared scale 1/n\u00b2 when taking the variance of the sum.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["covariance-bridge", "mean-derivation"]}, {"text": "Expand the sum variance into n\u03c3\u00b2 plus twice the sum of distinct-pair covariances.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["covariance-bridge", "mean-derivation"]}, {"text": "Specialize the variance expression to independent observations by setting covariances to zero and simplify to \u03c3\u00b2/n.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["mean-derivation", "se-formula"]}]}, {"id": "clt-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "When does the shape become normal?", "level": 2}, {"id": "clt-statement", "type": "prose", "text": "For independent identically distributed observations with finite mean and finite nonzero variance, the standardised mean $\\sqrt n(\\bar X-\\mu)/\\sigma$ converges in distribution to a standard normal as n increases. This is the iid central limit theorem. It concerns the distribution of a statistic across samples, not a promise that individual observations become normal.\n\nIf the population itself is normal, the sample mean is exactly normal for every n. For a skewed population, normality of the mean is an approximation whose quality depends on the distribution and the region of interest. There is no universal sample size that makes every tail probability accurate. Strong dependence or infinite variance can invalidate the theorem stated here.", "objectives": ["1.5.1"]}, {"id": "clt-reconstruct", "type": "question", "questionKind": "written", "prompt": "Without the displayed theorem, state the iid central limit theorem for a sample mean: specify the assumptions, the standardized random variable and its limiting distribution. Explain whether it makes individual observations normal, whether it is exact for a finite sample from a skewed population, and why a population with infinite variance is outside this theorem. Finally distinguish the normal-population case. [7 marks]", "solution": "For independent identically distributed X\u2081,\u2026,X\u2099 with finite common mean \u03bc and finite nonzero variance \u03c3\u00b2, \u221an(X\u0304\u2212\u03bc)/\u03c3 converges in distribution to N(0,1) as n\u2192\u221e. [2 assumptions; 2 standardized variable and limiting law.] This concerns the sampling distribution of a mean, not the distribution of individual observations. For a skewed population the finite-n normal approximation is not exact, with accuracy depending on the population and probability region. [1] Infinite variance violates the finite-variance hypothesis, so this stated theorem cannot justify the approximation. [1] If the population is normal, X\u0304 itself has the exact N(\u03bc,\u03c3\u00b2/n) distribution at every n, without waiting for a limit. [1]", "taughtIn": ["distribution-story", "clt-statement"], "objectives": ["1.5.1"], "rubric": [{"text": "State iid observations, finite mean and finite nonzero variance.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Give \u221an(X\u0304\u2212\u03bc)/\u03c3 and convergence in distribution to N(0,1) as n\u2192\u221e.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Distinguish individual observations from repeated-sample means and identify finite-n normality as approximate for a skewed population.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["distribution-story", "clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Identify infinite variance as failure of a stated hypothesis.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Distinguish exact normality of the mean for a normal population at every n.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["clt-statement"]}]}, {"id": "sampling-lab", "type": "lab", "labKind": "sampling", "title": "Build a distribution of means", "objectives": ["1.5.1", "1.5.2", "2.2.3", "2.2.4.mean"], "purpose": "Compare the population shape with the variability and approximate shape of repeated sample means.", "prediction": "Before drawing, predict what happens to the centre, spread and skew when sample size changes from 4 to 64 for an exponential population.", "debrief": "The empirical histogram is finite simulation evidence. Compare its spread with \u03c3/\u221an. The normal overlay is a theoretical reference, not a fit guaranteed by a smooth-looking histogram. Use Compare spread for a shared horizontal scale. Then choose Inspect shape at each n to examine the normal reference; this view changes the horizontal scale, so its widths cannot be compared across n.", "fallback": "For an exponential population with mean and standard deviation 10, the theoretical mean of every sample mean is 10. Its standard error is 5 at n=4 and 1.25 at n=64. Averaging reduces right skew, although finite-n means are not exactly normal.", "transfer": "Would taking 64 neighbouring correlated records justify the same standard error? Explain which step of the variance argument would change."}, {"id": "simulation-reporting", "type": "prose", "text": "Report a simulation as an observation, not as a theorem: name the population, n, number of repetitions and seed, then compare the empirical standard deviation of the generated means with \u03c3/\u221an. If the lab does not display the seed or repetition count, say that those settings were not exposed; do not invent them. Compare the histogram with the normal reference and describe what you actually see. Finite empirical spread need not equal the theoretical value.\n\nThe following is an actual separate reference computation, not a run of the on-screen lab. Algorithm: Python random.Random(2710), reset to that seed for each n; draw independent exponential values with random.expovariate(0.1), average n values, repeat 2,000 times, and compute empirical standard deviation with divisor 1,999 (statistics.stdev). For n=4, the empirical centre was 10.269042 and spread 5.051620, versus theoretical centre 10 and spread 5. For n=64, they were 10.005911 and 1.229425, versus 10 and 1.25. The differences are finite-simulation variation. These computed summaries do not themselves establish a histogram shape or validate real-world sampling assumptions; inspect the lab plot separately. Other algorithms, seeds or repetition counts can produce different values.", "objectives": ["1.5.2"]}, {"id": "clt-transfer", "type": "question", "prompt": "First use the sampling lab to generate exponential sample means at n=4 and n=64. Use Compare spread to compare their horizontal spread, then Inspect shape at each n to examine the normal reference on its fitted scale. Record each empirical spread and any displayed simulation seed/repetition count (say if unavailable), compare each with its theoretical standard error, and comment on the normal reference shape without treating one finite histogram as proof. Then a colleague says: \u201cAt n = 100 all individual costs are approximately normal, and the variance of the mean must be \u03c3\u00b2/100 even if the records are correlated.\u201d Identify two separate errors.", "solution": "An honest report gives the two empirical spreads actually observed, settings where available, comparison with theoretical standard errors 5 and 1.25, and a description of the visible shapes. As a labelled computed example, the separate reference computation in the reporting example above used random.Random(2710), reset for each n, exponential rate 0.1 and 2,000 repetitions: spreads were 5.051620 at n=4 and 1.229425 at n=64. These are example results, not required lab outputs. Reproduce the comparison with your own run; describe its actual histogram rather than copying an expected shape. The n=64 means are typically less skewed, but one finite plot is not proof. The colleague\u2019s first error confuses a sampling distribution with individual costs. The second discards covariance contributions: independence removes them from the sum variance, and a large n does not do so automatically.", "taughtIn": ["distribution-story", "mean-derivation", "clt-statement", "sampling-lab", "simulation-reporting"], "objectives": ["1.5.1", "1.5.2", "2.2.2"], "questionKind": "written", "rubric": [{"text": "Report both actual empirical spreads and available settings, compare with theoretical spread and the observed normal-reference shape, and acknowledge finite simulation variation.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["sampling-lab", "clt-statement", "simulation-reporting"]}, {"text": "Distinguish individual observations from the sampling distribution of a mean.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["distribution-story", "clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Explain the independence/covariance issue rather than relying on sample size.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["mean-derivation"]}]}, {"id": "sampling-readiness", "type": "callout", "title": "Choose a repair, then retry", "tone": "idea", "text": "If you used \u03c3\u00b2/n as a standard error, return to \u201cWhy averaging changes spread\u201d and keep variance in squared units. If you removed covariance merely because n is large, revisit the identical-copy check in the covariance bridge. If you made individual observations normal, revisit \u201cWhen does the shape become normal?\u201d Then retry without the formula: with \u03c3=15 and n=25, explain why the independent-mean standard error is 3, whereas identical copies have standard error 15. State the CLT assumptions aloud before using any normal approximation. Revealing feedback or moving controls does not establish mastery."}], "summary": ["A statistic is random before data are observed; its realised value is a number.", "For an iid finite-variance model, the sample mean has mean \u03bc and variance \u03c3\u00b2/n.", "The central limit theorem describes repeated-sample means, with assumptions and approximation limits.", "Simulation helps inspect a model; it does not verify that the real sampling design satisfies the model."], "coverage": [{"objective": "2.2.1", "teaching": ["population-story"], "practice": ["objects-check"]}, {"objective": "2.2.2", "teaching": ["population-story", "sampling-warning"], "practice": ["clt-transfer"]}, {"objective": "2.2.3", "teaching": ["distribution-story"], "practice": ["objects-check"]}, {"objective": "2.2.4.mean", "teaching": ["covariance-bridge", "mean-derivation", "se-formula"], "practice": ["se-practice", "mean-reconstruct"]}, {"objective": "1.5.1", "teaching": ["clt-statement"], "practice": ["clt-reconstruct", "clt-transfer"]}, {"objective": "1.5.2", "teaching": ["sampling-lab", "simulation-reporting"], "practice": ["clt-transfer"]}], "provenance": "Original prose, fictional situations, numerical designs, questions and interaction brief created independently for Open Actuary. The supplied 2019 syllabus identifies scope; no ActEd text, fictional case, exercise or diagram is reproduced.", "technicalNotes": ["Known population moments are model inputs; the finite simulation is generated with a reproducible seed.", "Mean/variance formulas can be checked independently by linearity and covariance expansion.", "Normal approximation and standard-error calculations are separated; exact normality requires a normal population."]}','5','{"revision": 5, "reviewer": "Independent teaching, interaction and browser reviewers; root recomputation and repair audit (2026-10-27)", "level": "pilot", "checks": {"technical": "Covariance expansion, identical-copy boundary, CLT conditions and sample-mean scaling checked. Separate Python Random(2710), 2000-draw reference reproduced independently; structural/KaTeX checks pass.", "pedagogy": "Fresh teaching review identified outcome/practice gaps; independent final audit verifies repaired prompts actually elicit outcomes and every rubric criterion has earlier teaching. Readiness routes and non-overlapping credit checked. Agent review only, no learner trial.", "interaction": "Local browser: compare-spread/inspect-shape toggles retain n64, seed43 and SD1.303; reset restores spread/n4/seed42. Geometric tests verify scales include all values. Final two-sentence content guidance follows tested modes.", "accessibility": "Independent actual desktop/mobile browser QA: new practice text wraps without page overflow at390px; HTML chart labels12px; navigation targets focus and preserve controls; search/header checked320px. Text/table alternatives retained. No assistive-technology-user validation claimed.", "originality": "Original historical-syllabus pilot and independently developed new scenarios/teaching. Review used current course snapshots and independent arithmetic, without copying reference-book expression. No external similarity scan or human copyright certification claimed."}, "unresolved": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-27T16:39:01.886Z"}','2026-10-27T16:40:32.662Z','{"revision": 5, "reviewer": "Independent teaching, interaction and browser reviewers; root recomputation and repair audit (2026-10-27)", "level": "pilot", "checks": {"technical": "Covariance expansion, identical-copy boundary, CLT conditions and sample-mean scaling checked. Separate Python Random(2710), 2000-draw reference reproduced independently; structural/KaTeX checks pass.", "pedagogy": "Fresh teaching review identified outcome/practice gaps; independent final audit verifies repaired prompts actually elicit outcomes and every rubric criterion has earlier teaching. Readiness routes and non-overlapping credit checked. Agent review only, no learner trial.", "interaction": "Local browser: compare-spread/inspect-shape toggles retain n64, seed43 and SD1.303; reset restores spread/n4/seed42. Geometric tests verify scales include all values. Final two-sentence content guidance follows tested modes.", "accessibility": "Independent actual desktop/mobile browser QA: new practice text wraps without page overflow at390px; HTML chart labels12px; navigation targets focus and preserve controls; search/header checked320px. Text/table alternatives retained. No assistive-technology-user validation claimed.", "originality": "Original historical-syllabus pilot and independently developed new scenarios/teaching. Review used current course snapshots and independent arithmetic, without copying reference-book expression. No external similarity scan or human copyright certification claimed."}, "unresolved": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-27T16:39:01.886Z"}');
INSERT INTO revisions VALUES('sampling-means','5','{"id": "sampling-means", "courseId": "cs1-inference", "title": "A sample mean is a moving target", "subtitle": "One population. Many possible samples. A distribution of answers.", "minutes": 30, "prerequisites": ["Expectation, variance, independence and the shape of a normal distribution.", "Summing finite sequences and interpreting a probability density."], "outcomes": ["Separate individual variability from variability of a mean.", "Derive standard error and explain what changes with sample size.", "Use simulation to challenge, rather than replace, the assumptions of the central limit theorem."], "objectiveIds": ["2.2.1", "2.2.2", "2.2.3", "2.2.4.mean", "1.5.1", "1.5.2"], "scopeNote": "Selected historical CS1 scope only. Sample-variance distributions, t and F methods are deferred.", "blocks": [{"id": "opening-question", "type": "callout", "title": "Before we calculate", "text": "An insurer cannot examine every administration transaction. It samples 25 records and estimates the average processing cost. If another team takes a different sample, should it obtain the same answer? The population can stay unchanged while the estimate moves.", "tone": "idea", "objectives": []}, {"id": "population-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "The object that stays fixed, and the object that changes", "level": 2}, {"id": "population-story", "type": "prose", "text": "The population is the collection or probability model about which we want to learn. A sample is the observed part we use. Statistical inference uses the sample to make a statement about a population quantity, such as its mean. A parameter belongs to the population model; a statistic is a function of the sample.\n\nLet $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be processing costs from an independent random sample with common mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$. Before observation, these symbols denote random variables. After sampling, their realised values are numbers. The independence assumption says that knowing one cost does not alter the distribution of another. It is a substantive assumption about selection and dependence, not a reward for collecting many records.", "objectives": ["2.2.1", "2.2.2", "2.2.3"]}, {"id": "sampling-warning", "type": "callout", "title": "A large sample can repeat a selection error", "text": "If only transactions completed during quiet shifts are sampled, the result may not represent all shifts. More records from the same selected group can shrink sampling variation while leaving the selection problem. The calculations below assume the sample really comes from the stated common population.", "tone": "warning", "objectives": ["2.2.2"]}, {"id": "distribution-story", "type": "prose", "text": "The sample mean is $\\bar X=(X_1+\\cdots+X_n)/n$. Once we see the sample, its value is a number. Before we see it, it is random because it is calculated from random inputs. Imagine repeating the same sampling plan many times without changing the population. The distribution of the resulting means is the sampling distribution of $\\bar X$.\n\nThis is a different distribution from that of individual costs. A histogram of costs tells us how transactions differ. A histogram of means tells us how a particular estimation procedure differs between repeated samples. A histogram from one observed sample is neither a census of the population nor, by itself, a sampling distribution.", "objectives": ["2.2.3"]}, {"id": "objects-check", "type": "question", "prompt": "A team draws 40 new records every week and plots each week\u2019s mean cost. If the population and sampling plan remain unchanged, which distribution is the plot trying to approximate?", "solution": "It approximates the sampling distribution of the mean for samples of size 40. Each plotted point is an entire sample summarised by one statistic, not one transaction.", "taughtIn": ["distribution-story"], "objectives": ["2.2.1", "2.2.3"], "questionKind": "choice", "options": ["The distribution of individual transaction costs", "The sampling distribution of the sample mean", "The probability distribution of a fixed population parameter"], "correct": 1, "feedback": ["Each point is a mean of 40 records, not an individual record.", "Yes. Repeating the sampling procedure makes the statistic vary.", "The parameter is fixed in this model; our statistic is random."]}, {"id": "derive-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "Why averaging changes spread", "level": 2}, {"id": "covariance-bridge", "type": "prose", "text": "Covariance describes how two random deviations move together. For finite second moments, Cov(X,Y)=E[(X\u2212E[X])(Y\u2212E[Y])]. A positive value means same-sign deviations contribute more on average than opposite-sign deviations; independence makes covariance zero, although zero covariance alone does not establish independence. Covariance has the product of the two variables\u2019 units.\n\nTo see why it enters, write U=X\u2212E[X] and V=Y\u2212E[Y]. Then Var(X+Y)=E[(U+V)\u00b2]=E[U\u00b2]+2E[UV]+E[V\u00b2]=Var(X)+2Cov(X,Y)+Var(Y). Each pair of different variables contributes twice when the squared sum is expanded. Dividing a sum by n scales its variance by 1/n\u00b2. These are the two ingredients used next.\n\nBoundary check: suppose X\u2081=\u22ef=X\u2099=Y, all copies of the same random outcome with variance \u03c3\u00b2>0. Then X\u0304=Y and Var(X\u0304)=\u03c3\u00b2, regardless of n. The covariance formula agrees: every distinct pair has covariance \u03c3\u00b2, giving [n\u03c3\u00b2+n(n\u22121)\u03c3\u00b2]/n\u00b2=\u03c3\u00b2. A larger row count cannot create independent information.", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"]}, {"id": "mean-derivation", "type": "prose", "text": "Linearity of expectation gives $E[\\bar X]=(E[X_1]+\\cdots+E[X_n])/n=\\mu$. Independence is not needed for this equality when the common mean exists. For variance, multiplying a variable by $1/n$ multiplies its variance by $1/n^2$. Independence removes covariance terms, so $\\operatorname{Var}(X_1+\\cdots+X_n)=n\\sigma^2$. Combining the two steps gives the formula below.\n\nIf the observations are positively correlated, omitted covariance terms can make the actual variance larger. It is therefore wrong to use the independent-sample formula merely because a sample contains n rows.\n\nIn general, Var(\u2211X\u1d62)=\u2211Var(X\u1d62)+2\u2211\u1d62<\u2c7c Cov(X\u1d62,X\u2c7c): the second sum includes each distinct pair once, with a factor of two. Combining this with the scale 1/n\u00b2 gives the correlated-sample expression. Independence sets the pairwise covariances to zero, rather than changing linearity of expectation.", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"]}, {"id": "se-formula", "type": "math", "tex": "E[\\bar X]=\\mu,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n},\\qquad \\operatorname{SD}(\\bar X)=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}", "explanation": "A mean stays centred on the population mean, while its standard deviation decreases with the square root of sample size. This standard deviation is called its standard error.", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"]}, {"id": "se-example", "type": "worked", "title": "How much information does a larger sample buy?", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"], "steps": [{"title": "Define the model", "text": "Suppose individual processing costs have mean 100 and standard deviation 20 currency units, and observations are independent.", "why": "Individual standard deviation describes cost variability, not uncertainty in a sample mean."}, {"title": "Calculate the first standard error", "text": "For n = 25, standard error = 20 / \u221a25 = 4 currency units.", "why": "Taking a mean averages some individual variation away."}, {"title": "Compare, rather than memorise", "text": "For n = 100, standard error = 20 / \u221a100 = 2. Four times as many observations halves the standard error.", "why": "To halve 1/\u221an, multiply n by four. Doubling n only multiplies the standard error by 1/\u221a2."}, {"title": "Check meaning and units", "text": "Both the mean and standard error are in currency units. The mean is still centred at 100. Larger n changes precision, not the population parameter.", "why": "A more precise estimate is not automatically unbiased if the sample design is wrong."}]}, {"id": "se-practice", "type": "question", "prompt": "Individual costs have standard deviation 18. What is the standard error of an independent sample mean based on 81 records? Give the answer in currency units.", "solution": "The standard error is 18/\u221a81 = 2. The variance is 4, which has squared units and is a different answer.", "taughtIn": ["mean-derivation", "se-formula", "se-example"], "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"], "questionKind": "numeric", "answer": 2, "tolerance": 0.001, "unit": "currency units", "hints": ["The question asks for standard deviation of the mean, not its variance.", "Use the square root of the sample size in the denominator."]}, {"id": "mean-reconstruct", "type": "question", "questionKind": "written", "objectives": ["2.2.4.mean"], "prompt": "Without looking at the displayed formula, reconstruct E[X\u0304] and Var(X\u0304) for n observations with common mean \u03bc and variance \u03c3\u00b2. State exactly where independence enters, and show what additional term appears when the observations have nonzero covariances.", "taughtIn": ["covariance-bridge", "mean-derivation", "se-formula"], "solution": "Write X\u0304=(1/n)\u2211X\u1d62. Linearity gives E[X\u0304]=(1/n)\u2211E[X\u1d62]=\u03bc; independence is unnecessary for that step. The variance of a scaled variable is multiplied by the square of the scale. Hence Var(X\u0304)=(1/n\u00b2)[\u2211Var(X\u1d62)+2\u2211\u1d62<\u2c7c Cov(X\u1d62,X\u2c7c)]. With common variance this is \u03c3\u00b2/n+(2/n\u00b2)\u2211\u1d62<\u2c7c Cov(X\u1d62,X\u2c7c). Independence makes every covariance zero, leaving \u03c3\u00b2/n. These identities explain why a large correlated sample need not have the independent-sample standard error. As a check on the general expression, identical copies X\u1d62=Y give Var(X\u0304)=\u03c3\u00b2, because the n(n\u22121)/2 equal covariance terms exactly supply the variation omitted by an independence assumption.", "rubric": [{"text": "Construct X\u0304=(1/n)\u2211X\u1d62 and derive its expectation \u03bc by linearity, which needs no independence.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["mean-derivation"]}, {"text": "Use the squared scale 1/n\u00b2 when taking the variance of the sum.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["covariance-bridge", "mean-derivation"]}, {"text": "Expand the sum variance into n\u03c3\u00b2 plus twice the sum of distinct-pair covariances.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["covariance-bridge", "mean-derivation"]}, {"text": "Specialize the variance expression to independent observations by setting covariances to zero and simplify to \u03c3\u00b2/n.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["mean-derivation", "se-formula"]}]}, {"id": "clt-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "When does the shape become normal?", "level": 2}, {"id": "clt-statement", "type": "prose", "text": "For independent identically distributed observations with finite mean and finite nonzero variance, the standardised mean $\\sqrt n(\\bar X-\\mu)/\\sigma$ converges in distribution to a standard normal as n increases. This is the iid central limit theorem. It concerns the distribution of a statistic across samples, not a promise that individual observations become normal.\n\nIf the population itself is normal, the sample mean is exactly normal for every n. For a skewed population, normality of the mean is an approximation whose quality depends on the distribution and the region of interest. There is no universal sample size that makes every tail probability accurate. Strong dependence or infinite variance can invalidate the theorem stated here.", "objectives": ["1.5.1"]}, {"id": "clt-reconstruct", "type": "question", "questionKind": "written", "prompt": "Without the displayed theorem, state the iid central limit theorem for a sample mean: specify the assumptions, the standardized random variable and its limiting distribution. Explain whether it makes individual observations normal, whether it is exact for a finite sample from a skewed population, and why a population with infinite variance is outside this theorem. Finally distinguish the normal-population case. [7 marks]", "solution": "For independent identically distributed X\u2081,\u2026,X\u2099 with finite common mean \u03bc and finite nonzero variance \u03c3\u00b2, \u221an(X\u0304\u2212\u03bc)/\u03c3 converges in distribution to N(0,1) as n\u2192\u221e. [2 assumptions; 2 standardized variable and limiting law.] This concerns the sampling distribution of a mean, not the distribution of individual observations. For a skewed population the finite-n normal approximation is not exact, with accuracy depending on the population and probability region. [1] Infinite variance violates the finite-variance hypothesis, so this stated theorem cannot justify the approximation. [1] If the population is normal, X\u0304 itself has the exact N(\u03bc,\u03c3\u00b2/n) distribution at every n, without waiting for a limit. [1]", "taughtIn": ["distribution-story", "clt-statement"], "objectives": ["1.5.1"], "rubric": [{"text": "State iid observations, finite mean and finite nonzero variance.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Give \u221an(X\u0304\u2212\u03bc)/\u03c3 and convergence in distribution to N(0,1) as n\u2192\u221e.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Distinguish individual observations from repeated-sample means and identify finite-n normality as approximate for a skewed population.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["distribution-story", "clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Identify infinite variance as failure of a stated hypothesis.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Distinguish exact normality of the mean for a normal population at every n.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["clt-statement"]}]}, {"id": "sampling-lab", "type": "lab", "labKind": "sampling", "title": "Build a distribution of means", "objectives": ["1.5.1", "1.5.2", "2.2.3", "2.2.4.mean"], "purpose": "Compare the population shape with the variability and approximate shape of repeated sample means.", "prediction": "Before drawing, predict what happens to the centre, spread and skew when sample size changes from 4 to 64 for an exponential population.", "debrief": "The empirical histogram is finite simulation evidence. Compare its spread with \u03c3/\u221an. The normal overlay is a theoretical reference, not a fit guaranteed by a smooth-looking histogram. Use Compare spread for a shared horizontal scale. Then choose Inspect shape at each n to examine the normal reference; this view changes the horizontal scale, so its widths cannot be compared across n.", "fallback": "For an exponential population with mean and standard deviation 10, the theoretical mean of every sample mean is 10. Its standard error is 5 at n=4 and 1.25 at n=64. Averaging reduces right skew, although finite-n means are not exactly normal.", "transfer": "Would taking 64 neighbouring correlated records justify the same standard error? Explain which step of the variance argument would change."}, {"id": "simulation-reporting", "type": "prose", "text": "Report a simulation as an observation, not as a theorem: name the population, n, number of repetitions and seed, then compare the empirical standard deviation of the generated means with \u03c3/\u221an. If the lab does not display the seed or repetition count, say that those settings were not exposed; do not invent them. Compare the histogram with the normal reference and describe what you actually see. Finite empirical spread need not equal the theoretical value.\n\nThe following is an actual separate reference computation, not a run of the on-screen lab. Algorithm: Python random.Random(2710), reset to that seed for each n; draw independent exponential values with random.expovariate(0.1), average n values, repeat 2,000 times, and compute empirical standard deviation with divisor 1,999 (statistics.stdev). For n=4, the empirical centre was 10.269042 and spread 5.051620, versus theoretical centre 10 and spread 5. For n=64, they were 10.005911 and 1.229425, versus 10 and 1.25. The differences are finite-simulation variation. These computed summaries do not themselves establish a histogram shape or validate real-world sampling assumptions; inspect the lab plot separately. Other algorithms, seeds or repetition counts can produce different values.", "objectives": ["1.5.2"]}, {"id": "clt-transfer", "type": "question", "prompt": "First use the sampling lab to generate exponential sample means at n=4 and n=64. Use Compare spread to compare their horizontal spread, then Inspect shape at each n to examine the normal reference on its fitted scale. Record each empirical spread and any displayed simulation seed/repetition count (say if unavailable), compare each with its theoretical standard error, and comment on the normal reference shape without treating one finite histogram as proof. Then a colleague says: \u201cAt n = 100 all individual costs are approximately normal, and the variance of the mean must be \u03c3\u00b2/100 even if the records are correlated.\u201d Identify two separate errors.", "solution": "An honest report gives the two empirical spreads actually observed, settings where available, comparison with theoretical standard errors 5 and 1.25, and a description of the visible shapes. As a labelled computed example, the separate reference computation in the reporting example above used random.Random(2710), reset for each n, exponential rate 0.1 and 2,000 repetitions: spreads were 5.051620 at n=4 and 1.229425 at n=64. These are example results, not required lab outputs. Reproduce the comparison with your own run; describe its actual histogram rather than copying an expected shape. The n=64 means are typically less skewed, but one finite plot is not proof. The colleague\u2019s first error confuses a sampling distribution with individual costs. The second discards covariance contributions: independence removes them from the sum variance, and a large n does not do so automatically.", "taughtIn": ["distribution-story", "mean-derivation", "clt-statement", "sampling-lab", "simulation-reporting"], "objectives": ["1.5.1", "1.5.2", "2.2.2"], "questionKind": "written", "rubric": [{"text": "Report both actual empirical spreads and available settings, compare with theoretical spread and the observed normal-reference shape, and acknowledge finite simulation variation.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["sampling-lab", "clt-statement", "simulation-reporting"]}, {"text": "Distinguish individual observations from the sampling distribution of a mean.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["distribution-story", "clt-statement"]}, {"text": "Explain the independence/covariance issue rather than relying on sample size.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["mean-derivation"]}]}, {"id": "sampling-readiness", "type": "callout", "title": "Choose a repair, then retry", "tone": "idea", "text": "If you used \u03c3\u00b2/n as a standard error, return to \u201cWhy averaging changes spread\u201d and keep variance in squared units. If you removed covariance merely because n is large, revisit the identical-copy check in the covariance bridge. If you made individual observations normal, revisit \u201cWhen does the shape become normal?\u201d Then retry without the formula: with \u03c3=15 and n=25, explain why the independent-mean standard error is 3, whereas identical copies have standard error 15. State the CLT assumptions aloud before using any normal approximation. Revealing feedback or moving controls does not establish mastery."}], "summary": ["A statistic is random before data are observed; its realised value is a number.", "For an iid finite-variance model, the sample mean has mean \u03bc and variance \u03c3\u00b2/n.", "The central limit theorem describes repeated-sample means, with assumptions and approximation limits.", "Simulation helps inspect a model; it does not verify that the real sampling design satisfies the model."], "coverage": [{"objective": "2.2.1", "teaching": ["population-story"], "practice": ["objects-check"]}, {"objective": "2.2.2", "teaching": ["population-story", "sampling-warning"], "practice": ["clt-transfer"]}, {"objective": "2.2.3", "teaching": ["distribution-story"], "practice": ["objects-check"]}, {"objective": "2.2.4.mean", "teaching": ["covariance-bridge", "mean-derivation", "se-formula"], "practice": ["se-practice", "mean-reconstruct"]}, {"objective": "1.5.1", "teaching": ["clt-statement"], "practice": ["clt-reconstruct", "clt-transfer"]}, {"objective": "1.5.2", "teaching": ["sampling-lab", "simulation-reporting"], "practice": ["clt-transfer"]}], "provenance": "Original prose, fictional situations, numerical designs, questions and interaction brief created independently for Open Actuary. The supplied 2019 syllabus identifies scope; no ActEd text, fictional case, exercise or diagram is reproduced.", "technicalNotes": ["Known population moments are model inputs; the finite simulation is generated with a reproducible seed.", "Mean/variance formulas can be checked independently by linearity and covariance expansion.", "Normal approximation and standard-error calculations are separated; exact normality requires a normal population."]}','2026-10-27T16:40:32.662Z','Migrated exact published snapshot from original Site');
INSERT INTO documents(id,kind,course_id,draft,revision,published,published_revision,review,updated_at,published_review) VALUES('testing-evidence','lesson','cs1-inference','{"id": "testing-evidence", "courseId": "cs1-inference", "title": "How surprising is the evidence?", "subtitle": "Build a decision rule before you look at the result.", "minutes": 35, "prerequisites": ["Independent normal sample means and their standard errors.", "Normal tail probabilities; known population standard deviation."], "outcomes": ["Define a null model, a pre-specified alternative and a significance level.", "Calculate a normal-mean test statistic and p-value and make a conditional decision.", "Distinguish Type I error, Type II error, power, and practical importance."], "objectiveIds": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "scopeNote": "Selected historical CS1 scope only. Known population standard deviation is assumed; estimating it requires methods beyond this unit.", "blocks": [{"id": "test-opening", "type": "callout", "title": "A change, or ordinary sampling variation?", "text": "An insurer has redesigned an administrative process. The previous model used mean cost 100 per record, with known standard deviation 20. A random sample of 25 records now has mean 106. Is that enough evidence of an increase? A difference in sample means alone does not tell us how unusual the result is under an unchanged population.", "tone": "idea", "objectives": []}, {"id": "test-model-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "Write down the question first", "level": 2}, {"id": "test-hypotheses", "type": "prose", "text": "Use the model of independent normal costs with known \u03c3=20. We test the simple null $H_0:\\mu=100$ against the one-sided alternative $H_1:\\mu>100$. The null is simple because it specifies one value for the unknown mean; the alternative is composite because it contains many possible means.\n\nThe direction should be chosen from the substantive question before examining the observed mean. If increases and decreases both matter, the alternative is $\\mu\\ne100$ and evidence in either tail counts. Choosing the tail after seeing which result looks more favourable changes the error properties of the procedure.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"]}, {"id": "test-rule", "type": "prose", "text": "Under the null, the statistic $Z=(\\bar X-100)/(20/\\sqrt n)$ is standard normal. A significance level \u03b1 is the probability of rejecting H\u2080 when this null model is true. For an upper-tail test, rejecting when Z exceeds the 1\u2212\u03b1 quantile creates an upper critical region with probability \u03b1 under the null.\n\nThe significance level belongs to the rule before sampling. The observed p-value instead measures how much null-model tail probability lies at least as extreme as the observed statistic, in the pre-specified direction. It is not the probability that the null is true.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"]}, {"id": "test-stat", "type": "math", "tex": "z_{\\mathrm{obs}}=\\frac{\\bar x-\\mu_0}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n},\\qquad p=1-\\Phi(z_{\\mathrm{obs}})\\quad\\text{(upper-tail test)}", "explanation": "Measure the observed mean in null-model standard errors, then use the corresponding upper tail of the standard normal distribution.", "objectives": ["3.3.2.normal-known"]}, {"id": "test-example", "type": "worked", "title": "A mean increase of six is not yet a decision", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "steps": [{"title": "Find the relevant scale", "text": "For n=25 and \u03c3=20, the standard error is 4. The observed statistic is (106\u2212100)/4 = 1.5.", "why": "The relevant variability is that of the mean, not the variability of an individual cost."}, {"title": "Find a tail probability", "text": "For the pre-specified upper alternative, p = 1\u2212\u03a6(1.5) \u2248 0.06681.", "why": "This is the chance, under \u03bc=100, of a sample mean at least as large as 106."}, {"title": "Apply the declared rule", "text": "At \u03b1=0.05, the critical Z value is about 1.64485. Since 1.5 is below it, or equivalently p>0.05, do not reject the null.", "why": "The observed result has not entered the pre-specified rejection region."}, {"title": "Use careful language", "text": "We have insufficient evidence of an increase at this level under the model. We have not proved that the mean is exactly 100.", "why": "Not rejecting a hypothesis is different from assigning it certainty or proving equivalence."}]}, {"id": "test-numeric", "type": "question", "prompt": "Use the same null and \u03c3=20, but n=100 and observed mean 106. What is the test statistic z?", "solution": "The standard error is 20/\u221a100=2, so z=(106\u2212100)/2=3. The upper-tail p-value is about 0.00135, which rejects at 5%. The observed difference stayed six, but its null-model scale changed.", "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-stat", "test-example"], "objectives": ["3.3.2.normal-known"], "questionKind": "numeric", "answer": 3, "tolerance": 0.001, "unit": "standard errors", "hints": ["Do not divide by the individual standard deviation of 20.", "Find the standard error of the mean for n=100."]}, {"id": "errors-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "Two ways a rule can be wrong", "level": 2}, {"id": "test-errors", "type": "prose", "text": "A Type I error rejects the null when it is true. For the exact model and continuous upper-tail rule above, its probability is \u03b1. A Type II error fails to reject when a specified alternative mean is true. Its probability depends on which alternative mean we choose. Power at that alternative is one minus its Type II error probability: the chance the rule detects the departure.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"]}, {"id": "power-worlds", "type": "table", "title": "One fixed rule, evaluated in two worlds", "headers": ["World", "Distribution of X\u0304 (n=25, \u03c3=20)", "Reject using the same cutoff", "Meaning of upper-tail probability"], "rows": [["Null: \u03bc=100", "Normal mean 100, standard error 4", "X\u0304>106.5794", "Type I error = 0.05"], ["Specified alternative: \u03bc=108", "Normal mean 108, standard error 4", "X\u0304>106.5794", "Power at 108 \u2248 0.63876"]], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"]}, {"id": "power-calculation", "type": "worked", "title": "Keep the rule fixed; change the probability model", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"], "steps": [{"title": "Choose the cutoff under H\u2080", "text": "At \u03b1=0.05 and n=25, the upper cutoff is c=100+z\u2080.\u2089\u2085\u00d74\u2248106.5794. This is fixed before sampling.", "why": "The null tail above c is 0.05, which sets the false-alarm probability."}, {"title": "Specify an alternative world", "text": "If \u03bc=108, X\u0304 has mean 108 and standard error 4. The rule still rejects only when X\u0304>c.", "why": "Power evaluates the original decision rule; it does not redesign its cutoff around 108."}, {"title": "Write the event before standardizing", "text": "Power at 108 = P\u2081\u2080\u2088(X\u0304>c)=1\u2212\u03a6((c\u2212108)/4)\u22480.63876. Type II error at 108 is the complementary probability, about 0.36124.", "why": "Subtract the mean of the world in which the probability is evaluated. The observed sample mean is not an input to this power calculation."}]}, {"id": "power-tradeoff", "type": "prose", "text": "Lowering \u03b1 while keeping n fixed makes false alarms less frequent under H\u2080, but pushes the upper cutoff outwards and reduces power against a given increase. Increasing a valid independent sample size shrinks the standard error and can improve detection of the same departure. A p-value uses observed data and the null world; power uses a specified alternative world and the whole rejection rule. Neither is the probability that a hypothesis is true. A very small p-value does not tell us whether a difference matters commercially: estimate the change, consider its uncertainty, and relate it to costs or decisions.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"]}, {"id": "power-completion", "type": "question", "questionKind": "written", "prompt": "Keep the original rule n=25, \u03c3=20, \u03bc\u2080=100, \u03b1=0.05 and c=106.5794. In the hypothetical world \u03bc=104, give the mean and standard error of X\u0304, write the power event, and complete its standardization. Use \u03a6(0.64485)\u22480.74049 to obtain power and Type II error. Is power higher or lower than at \u03bc=108, and why? [5 marks]", "solution": "Under \u03bc=104, X\u0304 is normal with mean 104 and standard error 4. [1] The unchanged rule gives P\u2081\u2080\u2084(X\u0304>106.5794). [1] Standardize about 104: (106.5794\u2212104)/4\u22480.64485. [1] Power\u22481\u22120.74049=0.25951 and Type II error\u22480.74049. [1] Power is lower than 0.63876 at 108: 104 lies farther below the same upper cutoff, with the same spread. [1] No observed sample mean or observed p-value was changed or needed.", "taughtIn": ["test-errors", "power-worlds", "power-calculation", "power-tradeoff"], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"], "rubric": [{"text": "Give alternative-world mean 104 and standard error 4.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["power-worlds", "power-calculation"]}, {"text": "Write P\u2081\u2080\u2084(X\u0304>106.5794), keeping the null-derived cutoff fixed.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["power-calculation"]}, {"text": "Standardize the cutoff about 104 to obtain approximately 0.64485.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["power-calculation"]}, {"text": "Obtain power 0.25951 and complementary Type II probability 0.74049.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["test-errors", "power-calculation"]}, {"text": "Explain lower detection at 104 than 108 through distance from the same cutoff and the same spread.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["power-worlds", "power-tradeoff"]}]}, {"id": "two-sided", "type": "prose", "text": "For a pre-specified two-sided alternative, departures in both directions count. Symmetry gives $p=2[1-\\Phi(|z_{\\mathrm{obs}}|)]$, and each critical tail receives \u03b1/2. At z=1.5 the two-sided p-value is about 0.13361. It is not appropriate to report the smaller one-sided p-value merely because the observed sign is positive.\n\nThese are normal known-variance tests. They do not validate the independence, sampling or normality assumptions. Unknown variance, paired data, other sampling models and likelihood-ratio tests require additional development and are deliberately deferred.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"]}, {"id": "testing-lab", "type": "lab", "labKind": "testing", "title": "Move the evidence and the decision rule separately", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "purpose": "Separate the observed p-value, the null-model critical region, and power at a specified alternative.", "prediction": "Keep the observed mean at 106. Predict what happens when n changes from 25 to 100. Then move the \u201ctrue mean for power\u201d control: should the observed p-value change?", "debrief": "The p-value uses the observed data and null model. Moving a hypothetical alternative mean changes power, not that observed p-value. Alpha changes the decision threshold, not the observed data. All curves here use the declared known-variance normal model.", "fallback": "With \u03bc\u2080=100, \u03c3=20 and x\u0304=106, an upper test gives z=1.5 and p\u22480.06681 at n=25, but z=3 and p\u22480.00135 at n=100. At n=25 and \u03b1=0.05, power at \u03bc=108 is about 0.6388. These are distinct probabilities answering distinct questions.", "transfer": "If the p-value is 0.04, explain why \u201cthe null has a 4% chance of being true\u201d is not justified, and what else is needed to assess practical importance."}, {"id": "test-interpret", "type": "question", "prompt": "An upper-tail test returns p=0.04 at a pre-specified 5% level. Which conclusion is justified?", "solution": "Reject the null using the declared rule, because 0.04\u22640.05. The p-value is a tail probability conditional on the null model, not its posterior probability, and does not measure the size or importance of a departure.", "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-example", "test-errors", "testing-lab", "power-tradeoff"], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"], "questionKind": "choice", "options": ["The null has a 4% chance of being true.", "The process has a commercially important increase with 96% certainty.", "The observed statistic lies in the declared rejection region under the model."], "correct": 2, "feedback": ["The p-value conditions on the null; it does not assign a probability to that hypothesis.", "Statistical evidence does not establish effect size or commercial importance.", "Correct. This is a conditional decision using the pre-specified rule."]}, {"id": "test-full", "type": "question", "questionKind": "written", "prompt": "A process has reference mean cost 50 currency units. Before data collection, the team asks specifically whether the mean has increased. An independent normal sample has known population \u03c3=12, n=36 and observed mean 53.5. At a pre-specified 5% significance level, state the hypotheses, calculate the standard error, z and upper-tail p-value, make the decision and interpret it in context. Use \u03a6(1.75)=0.95994. For a second team whose question was pre-specified as a change in either direction, use the same data to calculate the two-sided p-value and decision at 5%. Explain why the first team cannot choose a tail after seeing the result. [10 marks]", "solution": "For the first team H\u2080:\u03bc=50 and H\u2081:\u03bc>50, with direction fixed before seeing the sample. [2] SE=12/\u221a36=2 currency units and z=(53.5\u221250)/2=1.75. [2] The upper-tail p-value is 1\u22120.95994=0.04006, below 0.05, so reject H\u2080. [2] Under the stated sampling and normal model there is evidence of an increase at the 5% level; this does not prove an increase with 95% probability or establish commercial importance. [2] For a pre-specified two-sided H\u2081:\u03bc\u226050, p=2\u00d70.04006=0.08012, so do not reject at 5%. Selecting the one-sided rule after inspecting the sign changes the error properties and is not the declared two-sided procedure. [2]", "taughtIn": ["test-hypotheses", "test-rule", "test-stat", "test-example", "two-sided", "power-tradeoff"], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "rubric": [{"text": "State H\u2080:\u03bc=50 and the pre-specified upper H\u2081:\u03bc>50.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-hypotheses"]}, {"text": "Calculate SE=2 currency units and z=1.75.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-stat", "test-example"]}, {"text": "Calculate upper p=0.04006 and reject by comparison with 0.05.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-example"]}, {"text": "Give a conditional contextual evidence-of-increase conclusion without treating the test as a posterior probability or proof of practical importance.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-example", "power-tradeoff"]}, {"text": "Calculate two-sided p=0.08012, do not reject at 5%, and explain why data-dependent tail choice changes the procedure.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-hypotheses", "two-sided"]}]}, {"id": "test-transfer", "type": "question", "prompt": "A manager reduces \u03b1 from 5% to 1% but keeps the sample size fixed. Explain the likely tradeoff at a given alternative mean, then suggest how to improve detection without returning to a 5% false-alarm rate.", "solution": "A smaller \u03b1 moves the upper critical cutoff outward, reducing Type I error but also reducing power at a given upper alternative. Increasing a valid independent sample size reduces the standard error and can improve power while retaining \u03b1=1%. Neither choice repairs an invalid sampling model.", "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-errors", "two-sided", "power-worlds", "power-calculation", "power-tradeoff"], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "questionKind": "written", "rubric": [{"text": "Explain how a lower \u03b1 changes the cutoff and Type I error.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-errors", "power-tradeoff"]}, {"text": "Explain the resulting power/Type II tradeoff at a fixed alternative.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-errors", "power-tradeoff"]}, {"text": "Connect a larger valid sample to smaller standard error and improved power.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-errors", "test-stat", "power-tradeoff"]}]}, {"id": "testing-readiness", "type": "callout", "title": "Choose a repair, then retry", "tone": "idea", "text": "If you divided the observed difference by \u03c3 instead of \u03c3/\u221an, revisit the worked test. If you treated p as the probability of H\u2080, return to the definition of the null tail in \u201cWrite down the question first\u201d. If changing an alternative mean changed your p-value, return to \u201cOne fixed rule, evaluated in two worlds\u201d: power and observed evidence use different inputs. Retry without feedback: for reference 50, \u03c3=12, n=36 and observed mean 52, z=1 and \u03a6(1)=0.84134; explain why the upper p is 0.15866 and the two-sided p is 0.31732, both insufficient at 5%. Explain the conditioning, rather than copying a decision."}], "summary": ["Hypotheses and direction are declared before looking at the sample.", "A p-value measures null-model extremeness, not the probability that the null is true.", "Alpha controls Type I error for the rule; power depends on a specified alternative.", "A decision remains conditional on the model and does not establish practical importance."], "coverage": [{"objective": "3.3.1.normal-test", "teaching": ["test-hypotheses", "test-rule", "test-errors", "power-worlds", "power-calculation", "power-tradeoff"], "practice": ["test-interpret", "test-transfer", "power-completion", "test-full"]}, {"objective": "3.3.2.normal-known", "teaching": ["test-rule", "test-stat", "test-example", "two-sided"], "practice": ["test-numeric", "test-transfer", "test-full"]}], "provenance": "Original prose, fictional situations, numerical designs, questions and interaction brief created independently for Open Actuary. The supplied 2019 syllabus identifies scope; no ActEd text, fictional case, exercise or diagram is reproduced.", "technicalNotes": ["Normal CDF approximation is checked against independent reference values with tolerance.", "Known \u03c3 and exact normality distinguish this unit from estimated-variance and approximate tests.", "Power at \u03bc=108 is independently checked using the fixed null critical boundary."]}','2','{"id": "testing-evidence", "courseId": "cs1-inference", "title": "How surprising is the evidence?", "subtitle": "Build a decision rule before you look at the result.", "minutes": 35, "prerequisites": ["Independent normal sample means and their standard errors.", "Normal tail probabilities; known population standard deviation."], "outcomes": ["Define a null model, a pre-specified alternative and a significance level.", "Calculate a normal-mean test statistic and p-value and make a conditional decision.", "Distinguish Type I error, Type II error, power, and practical importance."], "objectiveIds": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "scopeNote": "Selected historical CS1 scope only. Known population standard deviation is assumed; estimating it requires methods beyond this unit.", "blocks": [{"id": "test-opening", "type": "callout", "title": "A change, or ordinary sampling variation?", "text": "An insurer has redesigned an administrative process. The previous model used mean cost 100 per record, with known standard deviation 20. A random sample of 25 records now has mean 106. Is that enough evidence of an increase? A difference in sample means alone does not tell us how unusual the result is under an unchanged population.", "tone": "idea", "objectives": []}, {"id": "test-model-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "Write down the question first", "level": 2}, {"id": "test-hypotheses", "type": "prose", "text": "Use the model of independent normal costs with known \u03c3=20. We test the simple null $H_0:\\mu=100$ against the one-sided alternative $H_1:\\mu>100$. The null is simple because it specifies one value for the unknown mean; the alternative is composite because it contains many possible means.\n\nThe direction should be chosen from the substantive question before examining the observed mean. If increases and decreases both matter, the alternative is $\\mu\\ne100$ and evidence in either tail counts. Choosing the tail after seeing which result looks more favourable changes the error properties of the procedure.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"]}, {"id": "test-rule", "type": "prose", "text": "Under the null, the statistic $Z=(\\bar X-100)/(20/\\sqrt n)$ is standard normal. A significance level \u03b1 is the probability of rejecting H\u2080 when this null model is true. For an upper-tail test, rejecting when Z exceeds the 1\u2212\u03b1 quantile creates an upper critical region with probability \u03b1 under the null.\n\nThe significance level belongs to the rule before sampling. The observed p-value instead measures how much null-model tail probability lies at least as extreme as the observed statistic, in the pre-specified direction. It is not the probability that the null is true.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"]}, {"id": "test-stat", "type": "math", "tex": "z_{\\mathrm{obs}}=\\frac{\\bar x-\\mu_0}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n},\\qquad p=1-\\Phi(z_{\\mathrm{obs}})\\quad\\text{(upper-tail test)}", "explanation": "Measure the observed mean in null-model standard errors, then use the corresponding upper tail of the standard normal distribution.", "objectives": ["3.3.2.normal-known"]}, {"id": "test-example", "type": "worked", "title": "A mean increase of six is not yet a decision", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "steps": [{"title": "Find the relevant scale", "text": "For n=25 and \u03c3=20, the standard error is 4. The observed statistic is (106\u2212100)/4 = 1.5.", "why": "The relevant variability is that of the mean, not the variability of an individual cost."}, {"title": "Find a tail probability", "text": "For the pre-specified upper alternative, p = 1\u2212\u03a6(1.5) \u2248 0.06681.", "why": "This is the chance, under \u03bc=100, of a sample mean at least as large as 106."}, {"title": "Apply the declared rule", "text": "At \u03b1=0.05, the critical Z value is about 1.64485. Since 1.5 is below it, or equivalently p>0.05, do not reject the null.", "why": "The observed result has not entered the pre-specified rejection region."}, {"title": "Use careful language", "text": "We have insufficient evidence of an increase at this level under the model. We have not proved that the mean is exactly 100.", "why": "Not rejecting a hypothesis is different from assigning it certainty or proving equivalence."}]}, {"id": "test-numeric", "type": "question", "prompt": "Use the same null and \u03c3=20, but n=100 and observed mean 106. What is the test statistic z?", "solution": "The standard error is 20/\u221a100=2, so z=(106\u2212100)/2=3. The upper-tail p-value is about 0.00135, which rejects at 5%. The observed difference stayed six, but its null-model scale changed.", "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-stat", "test-example"], "objectives": ["3.3.2.normal-known"], "questionKind": "numeric", "answer": 3, "tolerance": 0.001, "unit": "standard errors", "hints": ["Do not divide by the individual standard deviation of 20.", "Find the standard error of the mean for n=100."]}, {"id": "errors-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "Two ways a rule can be wrong", "level": 2}, {"id": "test-errors", "type": "prose", "text": "A Type I error rejects the null when it is true. For the exact model and continuous upper-tail rule above, its probability is \u03b1. A Type II error fails to reject when a specified alternative mean is true. Its probability depends on which alternative mean we choose. Power at that alternative is one minus its Type II error probability: the chance the rule detects the departure.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"]}, {"id": "power-worlds", "type": "table", "title": "One fixed rule, evaluated in two worlds", "headers": ["World", "Distribution of X\u0304 (n=25, \u03c3=20)", "Reject using the same cutoff", "Meaning of upper-tail probability"], "rows": [["Null: \u03bc=100", "Normal mean 100, standard error 4", "X\u0304>106.5794", "Type I error = 0.05"], ["Specified alternative: \u03bc=108", "Normal mean 108, standard error 4", "X\u0304>106.5794", "Power at 108 \u2248 0.63876"]], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"]}, {"id": "power-calculation", "type": "worked", "title": "Keep the rule fixed; change the probability model", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"], "steps": [{"title": "Choose the cutoff under H\u2080", "text": "At \u03b1=0.05 and n=25, the upper cutoff is c=100+z\u2080.\u2089\u2085\u00d74\u2248106.5794. This is fixed before sampling.", "why": "The null tail above c is 0.05, which sets the false-alarm probability."}, {"title": "Specify an alternative world", "text": "If \u03bc=108, X\u0304 has mean 108 and standard error 4. The rule still rejects only when X\u0304>c.", "why": "Power evaluates the original decision rule; it does not redesign its cutoff around 108."}, {"title": "Write the event before standardizing", "text": "Power at 108 = P\u2081\u2080\u2088(X\u0304>c)=1\u2212\u03a6((c\u2212108)/4)\u22480.63876. Type II error at 108 is the complementary probability, about 0.36124.", "why": "Subtract the mean of the world in which the probability is evaluated. The observed sample mean is not an input to this power calculation."}]}, {"id": "power-tradeoff", "type": "prose", "text": "Lowering \u03b1 while keeping n fixed makes false alarms less frequent under H\u2080, but pushes the upper cutoff outwards and reduces power against a given increase. Increasing a valid independent sample size shrinks the standard error and can improve detection of the same departure. A p-value uses observed data and the null world; power uses a specified alternative world and the whole rejection rule. Neither is the probability that a hypothesis is true. A very small p-value does not tell us whether a difference matters commercially: estimate the change, consider its uncertainty, and relate it to costs or decisions.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"]}, {"id": "power-completion", "type": "question", "questionKind": "written", "prompt": "Keep the original rule n=25, \u03c3=20, \u03bc\u2080=100, \u03b1=0.05 and c=106.5794. In the hypothetical world \u03bc=104, give the mean and standard error of X\u0304, write the power event, and complete its standardization. Use \u03a6(0.64485)\u22480.74049 to obtain power and Type II error. Is power higher or lower than at \u03bc=108, and why? [5 marks]", "solution": "Under \u03bc=104, X\u0304 is normal with mean 104 and standard error 4. [1] The unchanged rule gives P\u2081\u2080\u2084(X\u0304>106.5794). [1] Standardize about 104: (106.5794\u2212104)/4\u22480.64485. [1] Power\u22481\u22120.74049=0.25951 and Type II error\u22480.74049. [1] Power is lower than 0.63876 at 108: 104 lies farther below the same upper cutoff, with the same spread. [1] No observed sample mean or observed p-value was changed or needed.", "taughtIn": ["test-errors", "power-worlds", "power-calculation", "power-tradeoff"], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"], "rubric": [{"text": "Give alternative-world mean 104 and standard error 4.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["power-worlds", "power-calculation"]}, {"text": "Write P\u2081\u2080\u2084(X\u0304>106.5794), keeping the null-derived cutoff fixed.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["power-calculation"]}, {"text": "Standardize the cutoff about 104 to obtain approximately 0.64485.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["power-calculation"]}, {"text": "Obtain power 0.25951 and complementary Type II probability 0.74049.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["test-errors", "power-calculation"]}, {"text": "Explain lower detection at 104 than 108 through distance from the same cutoff and the same spread.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["power-worlds", "power-tradeoff"]}]}, {"id": "two-sided", "type": "prose", "text": "For a pre-specified two-sided alternative, departures in both directions count. Symmetry gives $p=2[1-\\Phi(|z_{\\mathrm{obs}}|)]$, and each critical tail receives \u03b1/2. At z=1.5 the two-sided p-value is about 0.13361. It is not appropriate to report the smaller one-sided p-value merely because the observed sign is positive.\n\nThese are normal known-variance tests. They do not validate the independence, sampling or normality assumptions. Unknown variance, paired data, other sampling models and likelihood-ratio tests require additional development and are deliberately deferred.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"]}, {"id": "testing-lab", "type": "lab", "labKind": "testing", "title": "Move the evidence and the decision rule separately", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "purpose": "Separate the observed p-value, the null-model critical region, and power at a specified alternative.", "prediction": "Keep the observed mean at 106. Predict what happens when n changes from 25 to 100. Then move the \u201ctrue mean for power\u201d control: should the observed p-value change?", "debrief": "The p-value uses the observed data and null model. Moving a hypothetical alternative mean changes power, not that observed p-value. Alpha changes the decision threshold, not the observed data. All curves here use the declared known-variance normal model.", "fallback": "With \u03bc\u2080=100, \u03c3=20 and x\u0304=106, an upper test gives z=1.5 and p\u22480.06681 at n=25, but z=3 and p\u22480.00135 at n=100. At n=25 and \u03b1=0.05, power at \u03bc=108 is about 0.6388. These are distinct probabilities answering distinct questions.", "transfer": "If the p-value is 0.04, explain why \u201cthe null has a 4% chance of being true\u201d is not justified, and what else is needed to assess practical importance."}, {"id": "test-interpret", "type": "question", "prompt": "An upper-tail test returns p=0.04 at a pre-specified 5% level. Which conclusion is justified?", "solution": "Reject the null using the declared rule, because 0.04\u22640.05. The p-value is a tail probability conditional on the null model, not its posterior probability, and does not measure the size or importance of a departure.", "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-example", "test-errors", "testing-lab", "power-tradeoff"], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"], "questionKind": "choice", "options": ["The null has a 4% chance of being true.", "The process has a commercially important increase with 96% certainty.", "The observed statistic lies in the declared rejection region under the model."], "correct": 2, "feedback": ["The p-value conditions on the null; it does not assign a probability to that hypothesis.", "Statistical evidence does not establish effect size or commercial importance.", "Correct. This is a conditional decision using the pre-specified rule."]}, {"id": "test-full", "type": "question", "questionKind": "written", "prompt": "A process has reference mean cost 50 currency units. Before data collection, the team asks specifically whether the mean has increased. An independent normal sample has known population \u03c3=12, n=36 and observed mean 53.5. At a pre-specified 5% significance level, state the hypotheses, calculate the standard error, z and upper-tail p-value, make the decision and interpret it in context. Use \u03a6(1.75)=0.95994. For a second team whose question was pre-specified as a change in either direction, use the same data to calculate the two-sided p-value and decision at 5%. Explain why the first team cannot choose a tail after seeing the result. [10 marks]", "solution": "For the first team H\u2080:\u03bc=50 and H\u2081:\u03bc>50, with direction fixed before seeing the sample. [2] SE=12/\u221a36=2 currency units and z=(53.5\u221250)/2=1.75. [2] The upper-tail p-value is 1\u22120.95994=0.04006, below 0.05, so reject H\u2080. [2] Under the stated sampling and normal model there is evidence of an increase at the 5% level; this does not prove an increase with 95% probability or establish commercial importance. [2] For a pre-specified two-sided H\u2081:\u03bc\u226050, p=2\u00d70.04006=0.08012, so do not reject at 5%. Selecting the one-sided rule after inspecting the sign changes the error properties and is not the declared two-sided procedure. [2]", "taughtIn": ["test-hypotheses", "test-rule", "test-stat", "test-example", "two-sided", "power-tradeoff"], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "rubric": [{"text": "State H\u2080:\u03bc=50 and the pre-specified upper H\u2081:\u03bc>50.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-hypotheses"]}, {"text": "Calculate SE=2 currency units and z=1.75.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-stat", "test-example"]}, {"text": "Calculate upper p=0.04006 and reject by comparison with 0.05.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-example"]}, {"text": "Give a conditional contextual evidence-of-increase conclusion without treating the test as a posterior probability or proof of practical importance.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-example", "power-tradeoff"]}, {"text": "Calculate two-sided p=0.08012, do not reject at 5%, and explain why data-dependent tail choice changes the procedure.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-hypotheses", "two-sided"]}]}, {"id": "test-transfer", "type": "question", "prompt": "A manager reduces \u03b1 from 5% to 1% but keeps the sample size fixed. Explain the likely tradeoff at a given alternative mean, then suggest how to improve detection without returning to a 5% false-alarm rate.", "solution": "A smaller \u03b1 moves the upper critical cutoff outward, reducing Type I error but also reducing power at a given upper alternative. Increasing a valid independent sample size reduces the standard error and can improve power while retaining \u03b1=1%. Neither choice repairs an invalid sampling model.", "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-errors", "two-sided", "power-worlds", "power-calculation", "power-tradeoff"], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "questionKind": "written", "rubric": [{"text": "Explain how a lower \u03b1 changes the cutoff and Type I error.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-errors", "power-tradeoff"]}, {"text": "Explain the resulting power/Type II tradeoff at a fixed alternative.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-errors", "power-tradeoff"]}, {"text": "Connect a larger valid sample to smaller standard error and improved power.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-errors", "test-stat", "power-tradeoff"]}]}, {"id": "testing-readiness", "type": "callout", "title": "Choose a repair, then retry", "tone": "idea", "text": "If you divided the observed difference by \u03c3 instead of \u03c3/\u221an, revisit the worked test. If you treated p as the probability of H\u2080, return to the definition of the null tail in \u201cWrite down the question first\u201d. If changing an alternative mean changed your p-value, return to \u201cOne fixed rule, evaluated in two worlds\u201d: power and observed evidence use different inputs. Retry without feedback: for reference 50, \u03c3=12, n=36 and observed mean 52, z=1 and \u03a6(1)=0.84134; explain why the upper p is 0.15866 and the two-sided p is 0.31732, both insufficient at 5%. Explain the conditioning, rather than copying a decision."}], "summary": ["Hypotheses and direction are declared before looking at the sample.", "A p-value measures null-model extremeness, not the probability that the null is true.", "Alpha controls Type I error for the rule; power depends on a specified alternative.", "A decision remains conditional on the model and does not establish practical importance."], "coverage": [{"objective": "3.3.1.normal-test", "teaching": ["test-hypotheses", "test-rule", "test-errors", "power-worlds", "power-calculation", "power-tradeoff"], "practice": ["test-interpret", "test-transfer", "power-completion", "test-full"]}, {"objective": "3.3.2.normal-known", "teaching": ["test-rule", "test-stat", "test-example", "two-sided"], "practice": ["test-numeric", "test-transfer", "test-full"]}], "provenance": "Original prose, fictional situations, numerical designs, questions and interaction brief created independently for Open Actuary. The supplied 2019 syllabus identifies scope; no ActEd text, fictional case, exercise or diagram is reproduced.", "technicalNotes": ["Normal CDF approximation is checked against independent reference values with tolerance.", "Known \u03c3 and exact normality distinguish this unit from estimated-variance and approximate tests.", "Power at \u03bc=108 is independently checked using the fixed null critical boundary."]}','2','{"revision": 2, "reviewer": "Independent teaching, interaction and browser reviewers; root recomputation and repair audit (2026-10-27)", "level": "pilot", "checks": {"technical": "Independently recomputed complete test: SE2, z1.75, upper p.04005916, two-sided p.08011831. Fixed-cutoff power at104=.25951102 and108=.63876003; structural/KaTeX checks pass.", "pedagogy": "Fresh teaching review identified outcome/practice gaps; independent final audit verifies repaired prompts actually elicit outcomes and every rubric criterion has earlier teaching. Readiness routes and non-overlapping credit checked. Agent review only, no learner trial.", "interaction": "Testing model and fallback independently checked; observed p excludes alternative-power control. New power table/worked/completion sequence checked against same fixed null cutoff; existing interaction retained.", "accessibility": "Independent actual desktop/mobile browser QA: new practice text wraps without page overflow at390px; HTML chart labels12px; navigation targets focus and preserve controls; search/header checked320px. Text/table alternatives retained. No assistive-technology-user validation claimed.", "originality": "Original historical-syllabus pilot and independently developed new scenarios/teaching. Review used current course snapshots and independent arithmetic, without copying reference-book expression. No external similarity scan or human copyright certification claimed."}, "unresolved": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-27T16:39:05.974Z"}','2026-10-27T16:40:37.837Z','{"revision": 2, "reviewer": "Independent teaching, interaction and browser reviewers; root recomputation and repair audit (2026-10-27)", "level": "pilot", "checks": {"technical": "Independently recomputed complete test: SE2, z1.75, upper p.04005916, two-sided p.08011831. Fixed-cutoff power at104=.25951102 and108=.63876003; structural/KaTeX checks pass.", "pedagogy": "Fresh teaching review identified outcome/practice gaps; independent final audit verifies repaired prompts actually elicit outcomes and every rubric criterion has earlier teaching. Readiness routes and non-overlapping credit checked. Agent review only, no learner trial.", "interaction": "Testing model and fallback independently checked; observed p excludes alternative-power control. New power table/worked/completion sequence checked against same fixed null cutoff; existing interaction retained.", "accessibility": "Independent actual desktop/mobile browser QA: new practice text wraps without page overflow at390px; HTML chart labels12px; navigation targets focus and preserve controls; search/header checked320px. Text/table alternatives retained. No assistive-technology-user validation claimed.", "originality": "Original historical-syllabus pilot and independently developed new scenarios/teaching. Review used current course snapshots and independent arithmetic, without copying reference-book expression. No external similarity scan or human copyright certification claimed."}, "unresolved": [], "createdAt": "2026-10-27T16:39:05.974Z"}');
INSERT INTO revisions VALUES('testing-evidence','2','{"id": "testing-evidence", "courseId": "cs1-inference", "title": "How surprising is the evidence?", "subtitle": "Build a decision rule before you look at the result.", "minutes": 35, "prerequisites": ["Independent normal sample means and their standard errors.", "Normal tail probabilities; known population standard deviation."], "outcomes": ["Define a null model, a pre-specified alternative and a significance level.", "Calculate a normal-mean test statistic and p-value and make a conditional decision.", "Distinguish Type I error, Type II error, power, and practical importance."], "objectiveIds": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "scopeNote": "Selected historical CS1 scope only. Known population standard deviation is assumed; estimating it requires methods beyond this unit.", "blocks": [{"id": "test-opening", "type": "callout", "title": "A change, or ordinary sampling variation?", "text": "An insurer has redesigned an administrative process. The previous model used mean cost 100 per record, with known standard deviation 20. A random sample of 25 records now has mean 106. Is that enough evidence of an increase? A difference in sample means alone does not tell us how unusual the result is under an unchanged population.", "tone": "idea", "objectives": []}, {"id": "test-model-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "Write down the question first", "level": 2}, {"id": "test-hypotheses", "type": "prose", "text": "Use the model of independent normal costs with known \u03c3=20. We test the simple null $H_0:\\mu=100$ against the one-sided alternative $H_1:\\mu>100$. The null is simple because it specifies one value for the unknown mean; the alternative is composite because it contains many possible means.\n\nThe direction should be chosen from the substantive question before examining the observed mean. If increases and decreases both matter, the alternative is $\\mu\\ne100$ and evidence in either tail counts. Choosing the tail after seeing which result looks more favourable changes the error properties of the procedure.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"]}, {"id": "test-rule", "type": "prose", "text": "Under the null, the statistic $Z=(\\bar X-100)/(20/\\sqrt n)$ is standard normal. A significance level \u03b1 is the probability of rejecting H\u2080 when this null model is true. For an upper-tail test, rejecting when Z exceeds the 1\u2212\u03b1 quantile creates an upper critical region with probability \u03b1 under the null.\n\nThe significance level belongs to the rule before sampling. The observed p-value instead measures how much null-model tail probability lies at least as extreme as the observed statistic, in the pre-specified direction. It is not the probability that the null is true.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"]}, {"id": "test-stat", "type": "math", "tex": "z_{\\mathrm{obs}}=\\frac{\\bar x-\\mu_0}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n},\\qquad p=1-\\Phi(z_{\\mathrm{obs}})\\quad\\text{(upper-tail test)}", "explanation": "Measure the observed mean in null-model standard errors, then use the corresponding upper tail of the standard normal distribution.", "objectives": ["3.3.2.normal-known"]}, {"id": "test-example", "type": "worked", "title": "A mean increase of six is not yet a decision", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "steps": [{"title": "Find the relevant scale", "text": "For n=25 and \u03c3=20, the standard error is 4. The observed statistic is (106\u2212100)/4 = 1.5.", "why": "The relevant variability is that of the mean, not the variability of an individual cost."}, {"title": "Find a tail probability", "text": "For the pre-specified upper alternative, p = 1\u2212\u03a6(1.5) \u2248 0.06681.", "why": "This is the chance, under \u03bc=100, of a sample mean at least as large as 106."}, {"title": "Apply the declared rule", "text": "At \u03b1=0.05, the critical Z value is about 1.64485. Since 1.5 is below it, or equivalently p>0.05, do not reject the null.", "why": "The observed result has not entered the pre-specified rejection region."}, {"title": "Use careful language", "text": "We have insufficient evidence of an increase at this level under the model. We have not proved that the mean is exactly 100.", "why": "Not rejecting a hypothesis is different from assigning it certainty or proving equivalence."}]}, {"id": "test-numeric", "type": "question", "prompt": "Use the same null and \u03c3=20, but n=100 and observed mean 106. What is the test statistic z?", "solution": "The standard error is 20/\u221a100=2, so z=(106\u2212100)/2=3. The upper-tail p-value is about 0.00135, which rejects at 5%. The observed difference stayed six, but its null-model scale changed.", "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-stat", "test-example"], "objectives": ["3.3.2.normal-known"], "questionKind": "numeric", "answer": 3, "tolerance": 0.001, "unit": "standard errors", "hints": ["Do not divide by the individual standard deviation of 20.", "Find the standard error of the mean for n=100."]}, {"id": "errors-heading", "type": "heading", "text": "Two ways a rule can be wrong", "level": 2}, {"id": "test-errors", "type": "prose", "text": "A Type I error rejects the null when it is true. For the exact model and continuous upper-tail rule above, its probability is \u03b1. A Type II error fails to reject when a specified alternative mean is true. Its probability depends on which alternative mean we choose. Power at that alternative is one minus its Type II error probability: the chance the rule detects the departure.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"]}, {"id": "power-worlds", "type": "table", "title": "One fixed rule, evaluated in two worlds", "headers": ["World", "Distribution of X\u0304 (n=25, \u03c3=20)", "Reject using the same cutoff", "Meaning of upper-tail probability"], "rows": [["Null: \u03bc=100", "Normal mean 100, standard error 4", "X\u0304>106.5794", "Type I error = 0.05"], ["Specified alternative: \u03bc=108", "Normal mean 108, standard error 4", "X\u0304>106.5794", "Power at 108 \u2248 0.63876"]], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"]}, {"id": "power-calculation", "type": "worked", "title": "Keep the rule fixed; change the probability model", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"], "steps": [{"title": "Choose the cutoff under H\u2080", "text": "At \u03b1=0.05 and n=25, the upper cutoff is c=100+z\u2080.\u2089\u2085\u00d74\u2248106.5794. This is fixed before sampling.", "why": "The null tail above c is 0.05, which sets the false-alarm probability."}, {"title": "Specify an alternative world", "text": "If \u03bc=108, X\u0304 has mean 108 and standard error 4. The rule still rejects only when X\u0304>c.", "why": "Power evaluates the original decision rule; it does not redesign its cutoff around 108."}, {"title": "Write the event before standardizing", "text": "Power at 108 = P\u2081\u2080\u2088(X\u0304>c)=1\u2212\u03a6((c\u2212108)/4)\u22480.63876. Type II error at 108 is the complementary probability, about 0.36124.", "why": "Subtract the mean of the world in which the probability is evaluated. The observed sample mean is not an input to this power calculation."}]}, {"id": "power-tradeoff", "type": "prose", "text": "Lowering \u03b1 while keeping n fixed makes false alarms less frequent under H\u2080, but pushes the upper cutoff outwards and reduces power against a given increase. Increasing a valid independent sample size shrinks the standard error and can improve detection of the same departure. A p-value uses observed data and the null world; power uses a specified alternative world and the whole rejection rule. Neither is the probability that a hypothesis is true. A very small p-value does not tell us whether a difference matters commercially: estimate the change, consider its uncertainty, and relate it to costs or decisions.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"]}, {"id": "power-completion", "type": "question", "questionKind": "written", "prompt": "Keep the original rule n=25, \u03c3=20, \u03bc\u2080=100, \u03b1=0.05 and c=106.5794. In the hypothetical world \u03bc=104, give the mean and standard error of X\u0304, write the power event, and complete its standardization. Use \u03a6(0.64485)\u22480.74049 to obtain power and Type II error. Is power higher or lower than at \u03bc=108, and why? [5 marks]", "solution": "Under \u03bc=104, X\u0304 is normal with mean 104 and standard error 4. [1] The unchanged rule gives P\u2081\u2080\u2084(X\u0304>106.5794). [1] Standardize about 104: (106.5794\u2212104)/4\u22480.64485. [1] Power\u22481\u22120.74049=0.25951 and Type II error\u22480.74049. [1] Power is lower than 0.63876 at 108: 104 lies farther below the same upper cutoff, with the same spread. [1] No observed sample mean or observed p-value was changed or needed.", "taughtIn": ["test-errors", "power-worlds", "power-calculation", "power-tradeoff"], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"], "rubric": [{"text": "Give alternative-world mean 104 and standard error 4.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["power-worlds", "power-calculation"]}, {"text": "Write P\u2081\u2080\u2084(X\u0304>106.5794), keeping the null-derived cutoff fixed.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["power-calculation"]}, {"text": "Standardize the cutoff about 104 to obtain approximately 0.64485.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["power-calculation"]}, {"text": "Obtain power 0.25951 and complementary Type II probability 0.74049.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["test-errors", "power-calculation"]}, {"text": "Explain lower detection at 104 than 108 through distance from the same cutoff and the same spread.", "marks": 1, "taughtIn": ["power-worlds", "power-tradeoff"]}]}, {"id": "two-sided", "type": "prose", "text": "For a pre-specified two-sided alternative, departures in both directions count. Symmetry gives $p=2[1-\\Phi(|z_{\\mathrm{obs}}|)]$, and each critical tail receives \u03b1/2. At z=1.5 the two-sided p-value is about 0.13361. It is not appropriate to report the smaller one-sided p-value merely because the observed sign is positive.\n\nThese are normal known-variance tests. They do not validate the independence, sampling or normality assumptions. Unknown variance, paired data, other sampling models and likelihood-ratio tests require additional development and are deliberately deferred.", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"]}, {"id": "testing-lab", "type": "lab", "labKind": "testing", "title": "Move the evidence and the decision rule separately", "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "purpose": "Separate the observed p-value, the null-model critical region, and power at a specified alternative.", "prediction": "Keep the observed mean at 106. Predict what happens when n changes from 25 to 100. Then move the \u201ctrue mean for power\u201d control: should the observed p-value change?", "debrief": "The p-value uses the observed data and null model. Moving a hypothetical alternative mean changes power, not that observed p-value. Alpha changes the decision threshold, not the observed data. All curves here use the declared known-variance normal model.", "fallback": "With \u03bc\u2080=100, \u03c3=20 and x\u0304=106, an upper test gives z=1.5 and p\u22480.06681 at n=25, but z=3 and p\u22480.00135 at n=100. At n=25 and \u03b1=0.05, power at \u03bc=108 is about 0.6388. These are distinct probabilities answering distinct questions.", "transfer": "If the p-value is 0.04, explain why \u201cthe null has a 4% chance of being true\u201d is not justified, and what else is needed to assess practical importance."}, {"id": "test-interpret", "type": "question", "prompt": "An upper-tail test returns p=0.04 at a pre-specified 5% level. Which conclusion is justified?", "solution": "Reject the null using the declared rule, because 0.04\u22640.05. The p-value is a tail probability conditional on the null model, not its posterior probability, and does not measure the size or importance of a departure.", "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-example", "test-errors", "testing-lab", "power-tradeoff"], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test"], "questionKind": "choice", "options": ["The null has a 4% chance of being true.", "The process has a commercially important increase with 96% certainty.", "The observed statistic lies in the declared rejection region under the model."], "correct": 2, "feedback": ["The p-value conditions on the null; it does not assign a probability to that hypothesis.", "Statistical evidence does not establish effect size or commercial importance.", "Correct. This is a conditional decision using the pre-specified rule."]}, {"id": "test-full", "type": "question", "questionKind": "written", "prompt": "A process has reference mean cost 50 currency units. Before data collection, the team asks specifically whether the mean has increased. An independent normal sample has known population \u03c3=12, n=36 and observed mean 53.5. At a pre-specified 5% significance level, state the hypotheses, calculate the standard error, z and upper-tail p-value, make the decision and interpret it in context. Use \u03a6(1.75)=0.95994. For a second team whose question was pre-specified as a change in either direction, use the same data to calculate the two-sided p-value and decision at 5%. Explain why the first team cannot choose a tail after seeing the result. [10 marks]", "solution": "For the first team H\u2080:\u03bc=50 and H\u2081:\u03bc>50, with direction fixed before seeing the sample. [2] SE=12/\u221a36=2 currency units and z=(53.5\u221250)/2=1.75. [2] The upper-tail p-value is 1\u22120.95994=0.04006, below 0.05, so reject H\u2080. [2] Under the stated sampling and normal model there is evidence of an increase at the 5% level; this does not prove an increase with 95% probability or establish commercial importance. [2] For a pre-specified two-sided H\u2081:\u03bc\u226050, p=2\u00d70.04006=0.08012, so do not reject at 5%. Selecting the one-sided rule after inspecting the sign changes the error properties and is not the declared two-sided procedure. [2]", "taughtIn": ["test-hypotheses", "test-rule", "test-stat", "test-example", "two-sided", "power-tradeoff"], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "rubric": [{"text": "State H\u2080:\u03bc=50 and the pre-specified upper H\u2081:\u03bc>50.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-hypotheses"]}, {"text": "Calculate SE=2 currency units and z=1.75.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-stat", "test-example"]}, {"text": "Calculate upper p=0.04006 and reject by comparison with 0.05.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-example"]}, {"text": "Give a conditional contextual evidence-of-increase conclusion without treating the test as a posterior probability or proof of practical importance.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-example", "power-tradeoff"]}, {"text": "Calculate two-sided p=0.08012, do not reject at 5%, and explain why data-dependent tail choice changes the procedure.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-hypotheses", "two-sided"]}]}, {"id": "test-transfer", "type": "question", "prompt": "A manager reduces \u03b1 from 5% to 1% but keeps the sample size fixed. Explain the likely tradeoff at a given alternative mean, then suggest how to improve detection without returning to a 5% false-alarm rate.", "solution": "A smaller \u03b1 moves the upper critical cutoff outward, reducing Type I error but also reducing power at a given upper alternative. Increasing a valid independent sample size reduces the standard error and can improve power while retaining \u03b1=1%. Neither choice repairs an invalid sampling model.", "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-errors", "two-sided", "power-worlds", "power-calculation", "power-tradeoff"], "objectives": ["3.3.1.normal-test", "3.3.2.normal-known"], "questionKind": "written", "rubric": [{"text": "Explain how a lower \u03b1 changes the cutoff and Type I error.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-rule", "test-errors", "power-tradeoff"]}, {"text": "Explain the resulting power/Type II tradeoff at a fixed alternative.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-errors", "power-tradeoff"]}, {"text": "Connect a larger valid sample to smaller standard error and improved power.", "marks": 2, "taughtIn": ["test-errors", "test-stat", "power-tradeoff"]}]}, {"id": "testing-readiness", "type": "callout", "title": "Choose a repair, then retry", "tone": "idea", "text": "If you divided the observed difference by \u03c3 instead of \u03c3/\u221an, revisit the worked test. If you treated p as the probability of H\u2080, return to the definition of the null tail in \u201cWrite down the question first\u201d. If changing an alternative mean changed your p-value, return to \u201cOne fixed rule, evaluated in two worlds\u201d: power and observed evidence use different inputs. Retry without feedback: for reference 50, \u03c3=12, n=36 and observed mean 52, z=1 and \u03a6(1)=0.84134; explain why the upper p is 0.15866 and the two-sided p is 0.31732, both insufficient at 5%. Explain the conditioning, rather than copying a decision."}], "summary": ["Hypotheses and direction are declared before looking at the sample.", "A p-value measures null-model extremeness, not the probability that the null is true.", "Alpha controls Type I error for the rule; power depends on a specified alternative.", "A decision remains conditional on the model and does not establish practical importance."], "coverage": [{"objective": "3.3.1.normal-test", "teaching": ["test-hypotheses", "test-rule", "test-errors", "power-worlds", "power-calculation", "power-tradeoff"], "practice": ["test-interpret", "test-transfer", "power-completion", "test-full"]}, {"objective": "3.3.2.normal-known", "teaching": ["test-rule", "test-stat", "test-example", "two-sided"], "practice": ["test-numeric", "test-transfer", "test-full"]}], "provenance": "Original prose, fictional situations, numerical designs, questions and interaction brief created independently for Open Actuary. The supplied 2019 syllabus identifies scope; no ActEd text, fictional case, exercise or diagram is reproduced.", "technicalNotes": ["Normal CDF approximation is checked against independent reference values with tolerance.", "Known \u03c3 and exact normality distinguish this unit from estimated-variance and approximate tests.", "Power at \u03bc=108 is independently checked using the fixed null critical boundary."]}','2026-10-27T16:40:37.837Z','Migrated exact published snapshot from original Site');
